== Lie groups
<lie-groups>
#statement("Definition")[
Let $G$ be a Lie group, the #emph[Lie algebra] $"Lie"\(G\)$ of $G$ can be defined in the following equivalent ways

+ The vector space of left invariant vector fields

+ The tangent space at the identity $T_e G$

]
#statement("Definition")[
A #emph[one-parameter subgroup] is a group homomorphism $phi.alt : bb(R) arrow.r G$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
+ Lie groups are parallelizable

+ One parameter subgroups are precisely the maximal integral curves of the left invariant vector fields starting at $e$. So there is a one to one correspondence between One parameter subgroups of $G$ and $"Lie"\(G\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+ For any $0 eq.not X_e in T_e G$, we can define a vector field $X_g = g_(*) X_e$, this is a nonvanishing global section of the tangent bundle

+ Suppose $phi.alt : bb(R) arrow.r G$ is a one parameter subgroup, let $X_e = phi.alt'\(0\)$, then we have a left invariant vector field $X$ on $G$, think of $frac(partial, partial t)$ as a left invariant vector field on $bb(R)$, thus $phi.alt$ as Lie group homomorphism induces $phi.alt_(*) frac(partial, partial t)$ which is also a left invariant vector field and $phi.alt'\(s\)=\(d phi.alt\)_s frac(partial, partial t)\|_s = X_(phi.alt\(s\))$. Conversely, if $phi.alt : bb(R) arrow.r G$ is the maximal integral curve of some left invariant vector field $X$, suppose the global flow generated by $X$ is $phi : G times bb(R) arrow.r G$, then $phi\(e\,t\)= phi.alt\(t\)$, $phi.alt\(t + s\)= phi\(e\,t + s\)= phi\(phi\(e\,t\)\,s\)= phi\(phi.alt\(t\)\,s\)$, since $L_(phi.alt\(t\))$ is an isomorphism, thus $L_(phi.alt\(t\)) compose phi.alt$ is the maximal integral curve starting at $phi.alt\(t\)$, thus $phi\(phi.alt\(t\)\,s\)= phi.alt\(t\)phi.alt\(s\)$

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Lie correspondence])[
+ If $phi.alt : G arrow.r H$ is a homomorphism of Lie groups, then $d phi.alt : "Lie"\(G\)arrow.r "Lie"\(H\)$ or $\(d phi.alt\)_e : T_e G arrow.r T_e H$ is an homomorphism of Lie algebras. Suppose $H lt.eq G$ is a Lie subgroup, then $"Lie"\(H\)= T_e H lt.eq T_e G$

+ $d phi.alt : "Lie"\(G\)arrow.r "Lie"\(H\)$ or $\(d phi.alt\)_1 : T_1 G arrow.r T_1 H$ is an homomorphism of Lie algebras

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+

+ Suppose $X$ is a left invariant vector field on $G$, then $\(d phi.alt\)_g X_g =\(d phi.alt\)_g\(d L_g\)_1 X_1\(f\)= X_1\(f compose phi.alt compose L_g\)= X_1\(f compose phi.alt compose L_g\)=\(d L_(phi.alt\(g\))\)_1\(d phi.alt\)_1 X_1\(f\)$ which gives a left invariant vector field, thus using Lemma #ref(label("Pushforward of vector field"), supplement: none) #display-equation($\(d phi.alt\)\[X\,Y\]\(f\) & =\[X\,Y\]\(f compose phi.alt\)\
   & = X\(Y\(f compose phi.alt\)\)- Y\(X\(f compose phi.alt\)\)\
   & = X\(\(\(d phi.alt Y\)f\)compose phi.alt\)- Y\(\(\(d phi.alt X\)f\)compose phi.alt\)\
   & =\(\(d phi.alt X\)\(d phi.alt Y\)f\)compose phi.alt -\(\(d phi.alt Y\)\(d phi.alt X\)f\)compose phi.alt\
   & =\(\[d phi.alt X\,d phi.alt Y\]f\)compose phi.alt$) Therefore $\(d phi.alt\)\[X\,Y\]=\[\(d phi.alt X\)\,\(d phi.alt Y\)\]$, $d phi.alt$ is a Lie algebra homomorphism

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Suppose $\(pi\,V\)$ is a complex representation of a compact Lie group $G$, then there exists a positive definite Hermitian form such that this representation is unitary

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Choose any positive definite Hermitian form $chevron.l\,chevron.r$, define Hermitian form #display-equation($\(v\,w\):= integral_G chevron.l g v\,g w chevron.r d mu$) Here $mu$ is the Haar measure with $integral_G d mu = 1$, $\(\,\)$ is $G$ left invariant

]
#statement("Definition")[
Suppose $G$ acts on $M$, $G_p$ is the stablizer of $p$. The #emph[isotropy representation]#index-entry("Isotropy representation") is $G_p arrow.r G L\(T_p M\)$

]
#statement("Example")[
Consider Lie group $"SL"\(2\,bb(C)\)$ whose Lie algebra is $frak(s l)\(2\,bb(C)\)$, which is generated by $H = mat(delim: "(", 1, 0; 0, - 1)\,X = mat(delim: "(", 0, 1; 0, 0)\,Y = mat(delim: "(", 0, 0; 1, 0)$, thus $D_H = - x_1 frac(partial, partial x_1) + x_2 frac(partial, partial x_2)\,D_X = - x_2 frac(partial, partial x_1)\,D_Y = - x_1 frac(partial, partial x_2)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
For any $A in T_1 G$, define the exponential map $exp A := phi.alt_A\(1\)$ where $phi.alt_A : bb(R) arrow.r G$ is the one parameter subgroup corresponding to $A$, also it is easy to see that $exp t A := phi.alt_(t A)\(1\)= phi.alt_A\(t\)$ which is a scaling of the integral curve, and $exp\(t + s\)A = exp t A exp s A$ since $exp t A$ is a one parameter subgroup, and thus $\(exp A\)^(- 1) = exp\(- A\)$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
\(#emph[Properties of exponential map])

Let $G\,H$ be Lie groups with Lie algebras $frak(g)\,frak(h)$

#emph[\(a)] The exponential map is a smooth map

#emph[\(b)] $\(d exp\)_0 : frak(g) tilde.equiv T_0 frak(g) arrow.r T_1 G tilde.equiv frak(g)$ is the identity map, which implies that the exponential map is a local diffeomorphism around $0$

#emph[\(c)] Suppose $phi.alt : G arrow.r H$ is a Lie group homomorphism, then the following diagram commutes

