#statement("Definition")[
#index-entry("Nonassociative algebra") A #emph[nonassociative] $k$-#emph[algebra]#index-entry("Nonassociative k algebra") $A$ is an $k$ vector space with a multiplication $dot.op$ such that it is distributive $\(a + b\)dot.op c = a dot.op c + b dot.op c$, $a dot.op\(b + c\)= a dot.op b + a dot.op c$. $A$

- is #emph[unital] if $1 in A$

- is #emph[symmetric] if $x y = y x$

- is #emph[antisymmetric] if $x y = - y x$

- satisfies #emph[Jacobi identity]#index-entry("Jacobi identity") if $\(x y\)z +\(y z\)x +\(z x\)y = 0$

A homomorphism $phi.alt : A arrow.r B$ is a linear map such that $phi.alt\(x y\)= phi.alt\(x\)phi.alt\(y\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Suppose $e_1\,dots.h.c\,e_n$ is a basis of $A$, $e_i e_j = sum_i c_k^(i j) e_k$, $c_k^(i j)$ are called #emph[structure constants] with respect to $e_1\,dots.h.c\,e_n$. If $A$ satisfies Jacobi identity, then #display-equation($sum_l c_m^(i l) c_l^(j k) + sum_l c_m^(j l) c_l^(k i) + sum_l c_m^(k l) c_l^(i j) = 0$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
$B lt.eq A$ is a #emph[subalgebra] if $B$ is a subspace such that $B B subset.eq B$. $I lt.eq A$ is a #emph[left ideal] if $A I subset.eq I$. Suppose $I\,J lt.eq A$ are ideals, define #emph[ideal quotients] $\(J : I\)= { x in A\|x I subset.eq J }$ which is an ideal. Homomorphisms preserve ideals

]
#aside("Remark")[
If $A$ is (anti)symmetric, left ideals are two-sided ideals

]
#statement("Definition")[
$A$ is #emph[abelian] if $A A = 0$, $A$ is #emph[simple] if it is not abelian and the only ideals are $0$ and $A$, $A$ is #emph[semisimple] if $A = A_1 xor dots.h.c xor A_n$ is the direct sum of simple subalgebras, $A$ is #emph[reductive] if $A = frak(s) xor frak(a)$ is a direct sum of a semisimple subalgebra $frak(s)$ and an abelian subalgebra $frak(a)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
A #emph[derivation]#index-entry("Derivation") is an endomorphism $D : A arrow.r A$ such that $D\(a b\)= D\(a\)b + a D\(b\)$. Derivation algebra $"Der"_k\(A\)$ is the set of all derivations. If $D_1\,D_2 in "Der"_k\(A\)$, then $\[D_1\,D_2\]= D_1 D_2 - D_2 D_1 in "Der"_k\(A\)$, $"Der"_k\(A\)lt.eq "End"_k\(A\)$ is a Lie subalgebra

]
#statement("Definition")[
$R arrow.r^phi S$ is a ring homomorphism between commutative rings. The module of #emph[Kähler differentials]#index-entry("Kähler differentials") is $Omega_(S\/R)$ satisfies the universal property that any derivation uniquely factors through $d_(S\/R) : S arrow.r Omega_(S\/R)$, i.e. $"Hom"_S\(Omega_(S\/R)\,M\)tilde.equiv "Der"_R\(S\,M\)$. $Omega_(S\/R)$ can be constructed as #display-equation($frac({ d s : s in S }, d r = 0\,d\(s + t\)= d s + d t\,d\(s t\)= s d t + t d s)$) Another construction: define $I$ to be the kernel of $S times.o_R S arrow.r S$, $s times.o t mapsto s t$, and $Omega_(S\/R) = I\/I^2$, with $d s = 1 times.o s - s times.o 1$, the free $S$ module generated by $d s$ consists of $t times.o s - t s times.o 1$, which is the kernel of $S times.o_R S arrow.r S times.o_R R$, $t times.o s mapsto t s times.o 1$, moding $I^2$ is precisely the Leibniz rule because $d\(s t\)- s d t - t d s =\(1 times.o s t - s t times.o 1\)-\(s times.o t - s t times.o 1\)-\(t times.o s - t s times.o 1\)=\(1 times.o s - s times.o 1\)\(1 times.o t - t times.o 1\)in I^2$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$f : X arrow.r Y$ is a morphism of schemes, consider the diagonal $Delta : Y arrow.r X times_Y X$, let $I = ker Delta^(*)$, the #emph[cotangent sheaf]#index-entry("Cotangent sheaf") is $Omega_(X\/Y) = I\/I^2$, and a derivation $d : cal(O)_X arrow.r Omega_(X\/Y)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$A$ is an associative algebra. $M in frak(g l)_n\(A\)$, $P in "GL"_n\(A\)$, then $"Tr"\(P M P^(- 1)\)- "Tr"\(M\)in\[A\,A\]$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
#display-equation($"Tr"\(P M P^(- 1)\)- "Tr"\(M\) & = sum_(i\,j\,k) p_(i j) m_(j k) p^(k i) - sum_(k\,j) delta_(k j) m_(j k)\
 & = sum_(i\,j\,k) p_(i j) m_(j k) p^(k i) - sum_(i\,j\,k) p^(k i) p_(i j) m_(j k)\
 & = sum_(i\,j\,k)\(p_(i j) m_(j k) p^(k i) - p^(k i) p_(i j) m_(j k)\)\
 & = sum_(i\,j\,k)\[p_(i j) m_(j k)\,p^(k i)\]$)

]
