#statement("Definition")[
A #emph[Lie algebra]#index-entry("Lie algebra") $frak(g)$ is a anticommutative nonassociative $k$ algebra satisfying Jacobi identity, #emph[Lie bracket] $\[\,\]: frak(g) and frak(g) arrow.r frak(g)$ denotes the multiplication. If $upright(c h a r) k = 2$, we also require $\[x\,x\]= 0$

]
#statement("Definition")[
A $frak(g)$ #emph[module]#index-entry("\\mathfrak g module") $V$ is an abelian group with left group action $frak(g) times V arrow.r V$ such that $1 v = v$, $x\(v + w\)= x v + x w$, $\(x + y\)v = x v + y v$, $\(x y\)v = x\(y v\)- y\(x v\)$. Equivalently, a #emph[Lie algebra representation]#index-entry("Lie algebra representation") $\(pi\,V\)$ is a Lie algebra homomorphism $pi : frak(g) arrow.r frak(g l)\(V\)$, where $V$ is an $k$ vector space, $x v := pi\(x\)v$ give $V$ the $frak(g)$ module structure

]
#aside("Remark")[
A $frak(g)$ module is not a module according to Definition #ref(label("Module"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Definition")[
A $frak(g)$ #emph[module homomorphism] $phi.alt : V arrow.r W$ between $frak(g)$ modules is a group homomorphism such that $phi.alt\(x v\)= x phi.alt\(v\)$. Equivalently, an intertwine map $phi.alt : V arrow.r W$ between Lie algebra representations is a linear map such that $phi.alt\(pi_V\(x\)v\)= pi_W\(x\)phi.alt\(v\)$, giving the $frak(g)$ module homomorphism

A subrepresentation $\(pi\,W\)$ is a $frak(g)$ submodule $W lt.eq V$

]
#statement("Definition")[
The #emph[adjoint endomorphism]#index-entry("adjoint endomorphism") associated to $x$ is left multiplication by $x$, i.e. $a d\(x\)\(y\)=\[x\,y\]$, Jacobi identity becomes $a d\(\[x\,y\]\)=\[a d\(x\)\,a d\(y\)\]$, give a Lie algebra representation(adjoint representation) $a d : frak(g) arrow.r frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$, $a d\(x\)$ are called #emph[inner derivations]#index-entry("Inner derivations") since $a d\(z\)\[x\,y\]=\[a d\(z\)x\,y\]+\[x\,a d\(z\)y\]$. $a d\(frak(g)\)lt.eq "Der"\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$ are Lie subalgebras

Any Lie algebra homomorphism $phi.alt : frak(g) arrow.r frak(h)$ induce a Lie algebra homomorphism $phi.alt : a d\(frak(g)\)arrow.r a d\(frak(h)\)$ by $phi.alt\(a d\(x\)\)= a d\(phi.alt\(x\)\)$

#align(center)[
#align(center)[#set text(size:9pt)
#diagram(spacing:(7mm,9mm),
node((0,0),[$frak(g)$]),
node((1,0),[$frak(h)$]),
node((0,1),[$a d\(frak(g)\)$]),
node((1,1),[$a d\(frak(h)\)$]),
edge((0,0),(1,0),"->",[$phi.alt$]),
edge((0,0),(0,1),"->",[$a d$]),
edge((1,0),(1,1),"->",[$a d$]),
edge((0,1),(1,1),"->",[$phi.alt$]))]
]
]#label("Adjoint representation")
#statement("Definition")[
Centralizer of $S$ is defined to be $C_(frak(g))\(S\):= { g in frak(g)\|\[g\,S\]= 0 }$, in particular, the center $Z\(frak(g)\):= C_(frak(g))\(frak(g)\)$ \ Normalizer of $S$ is defined to be $N_(frak(g))\(S\):= { g in frak(g)\|\[g\,S\]subset.eq S }$ \

]
#statement("Definition")[
#display-equation($frak(g) supset.eq\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]supset.eq [\[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \] \, \[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \]] supset.eq [[\[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \] \, \[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \]] \, [\[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \] \, \[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \]]] supset.eq dots.h.c$) is called the #emph[derived series]#index-entry("Derived series"), $frak(g)$ is #emph[solvable]#index-entry("Solvable Lie algebra") if derived series terminates #display-equation($frak(g) supset.eq\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]supset.eq [frak(g) \, \[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \]] supset.eq [frak(g) \, [frak(g) \, \[ frak(g) \, frak(g) \]]] supset.eq dots.h.c$) is called the #emph[lower central series]#index-entry("Lower central series"), $frak(g)$ is #emph[nilpotent]#index-entry("Nilpotent Lie algebra") if lower central series terminates

]
#statement("Example")[
$\[frak(g l)\(V\)\,frak(g l)\(V\)\]= frak(s l)\(V\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Since $"Tr"\[X\,Y\]= 0$, thus $\[frak(g l)\(V\)\,frak(g l)\(V\)\]lt.eq frak(s l)\(V\)$, conversely

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $frak(g)$ be a Lie algebra, a Cartan subalgebra $frak(h) lt.eq frak(g)$ is a nilpotent subalgebra such that $N_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)= frak(h)$\(self normalizing or alternatively $\(frak(h) : frak(h)\)= frak(h)$)

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ be a Lie algebra, $frak(g)$ is called #emph[toral]#index-entry("Toral Lie algebra") if $frak(g)$ consists of semisimple elements

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $frak(g)$ be a Lie algebra, we can show the sum of all solvable ideal is again a solvable ideal, thus $frak(g)$ has a unique maximal solvable ideal $"rad"\(frak(g)\)$, called the #emph[radical]#index-entry("Radical ideal of Lie algebra") of $frak(g)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $frak(g)$ be a complex Lie algebra, $frak(g)_0$ is called a #emph[real form]#index-entry("Real form") of $frak(g)$ if $frak(g) tilde.equiv frak(g)_0 times.o_(bb(R)) bb(C)$

]
#statement("Example")[
Let $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ be Lie subalgebra, the #emph[tautological representation]#index-entry("tautological representation of Lie algebra") $\(tau\,V\)$ is defined by $tau\(x\)= x$, then $tau\(\[x\,y\]\)=\[x\,y\]=\[tau\(x\)\,tau\(y\)\]$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Lie algebra $frak(g)$ is reductive iff its adjoint representation is completely reducible

]
#statement("Lemma")[
If $frak(g)$ is semisimple Lie algebra, and $phi : frak(g) arrow.r frak(g l)\(V\)$ is a Lie algebra representation, then $phi\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(s l)\(V\)$

]#label("g semisimple, phi:g->gl(V) representation => phi(g)<=sl(V)")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
By Proposition #ref(label("g simple implies [g,g]=g"), supplement: none), $phi\(frak(g)\)= phi\(\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]\)lt.eq\[frak(g l)\(V\)\,frak(g l)\(V\)\]= frak(s l)\(V\)$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
If $frak(g)$ is a semisimple Lie algebra, then $a d\(frak(g)\)= D e r\(frak(g)\)$

]#label("Derivations of semisimple Lie algebra are inner derivations")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
As an abelian ideal of $frak(g)$, $Z\(frak(g)\)= 0$, thus $frak(g) arrow.r^(a d) a d\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$ is an embedding. Since $\[delta\,a d\(x\)\]= a d\(delta\(x\)\)\,delta in D e r\(frak(g)\)$, thus $\[D e r\(frak(g)\)\,a d\(frak(g)\)\]subset.eq a d\(frak(g)\)$, Let $K\(\,\)$ be the Killing form on $D e r\(frak(g)\)$, due to Proposition #ref(label("Equivalent conditions for semisimplicity"), supplement: none)\(b) and Proposition #ref(label("Some basic properties of Killing form"), supplement: none)\(c), $K\(\,\)\|_(a d\(g\))$ is nondegenerate, denote $I := a d\(frak(g)\)^perp$ under $K\(\,\)$, then $I inter a d\(frak(g)\)= 0$, otherwise $0 eq.not I inter a d\(frak(g)\)subset.eq ker K\(\,\)\|_(a d\(frak(g)\))$, by Exercise #ref(label("A is the direct sum of ideals => A is the product of these ideals"), supplement: none), $\[I\,a d\(frak(g)\)\]= 0$, thus for any $delta in I$, $0 =\[delta\,a d\(x\)\]= a d\(delta\(x\)\)$, since $a d$ is an isomorphism, $delta\(x\)= 0$, thus $delta = 0$, $I = 0$, $a d\(frak(g)\)= D e r\(frak(g)\)$

]
#aside("Remark")[
When $frak(g)$ is a semisimple Lie algebra, $frak(g) arrow.r^(a d) a d\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$ is an embedding, we can identify $x$ with $a d\(x\)$, by abuse of notations, $x y$ can defined to be the preimage of $a d\(x\)a d\(y\)in frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $G$ be a compact Lie group, we can pick any nonzero $k$-form $alpha_I$ at $I$, and extend it to a $k$-form on $G$ by $alpha_A\(Y_1\,dots.h.c\,Y_k\)= alpha_I\(Y_1 A^(- 1)\,dots.h.c\,Y_k A^(- 1)\)$, or just $R_A^(*) alpha_A = alpha_I$, then we can define integral $integral_G f\(A\)alpha$, then we would have $integral_G f\(A B\)alpha = integral_G f\(A B\)alpha_A = integral_G f\(A B\)R_B^(*) alpha_(A B) = integral_G f\(A\)R_B^(*) alpha = integral_G f\(A\)alpha$, since $R_B^(*) alpha = alpha$, i.e. $\(R_B^(*) alpha\)_A\(X_A\)= R_B^(*) alpha_(A B)\(X_A\)= alpha_A\(X_A\)$, thus this integration is right invariant. Note that this actually gives a right invariant Haar measure

]
#statement("Theorem")[
#emph[Weyl's theorem]#index-entry("Weyl’s theorem") \ Let $frak(g)$ be a semisimple Lie algebra and $phi : frak(g) arrow.r frak(g l)\(V\)$ is a Lie algebra representation, then $phi$ is completely reducible, namely, $frak(g)$ modules are semisimple(completely reducible), thus any irreducible subrepresentation has to be one of the summand in the decomposition

]#label("Weyl's theorem")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Weyl's unitary trick

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $V eq.not 0$ be a finite dimensional vector space, suppose $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ is a Lie subalgebra consists of nilpotent elements, then there exists $0 eq.not v in V$ such that $frak(g) v = 0$

]#label("Lemma for Engel's theorem")
#statement("Theorem")[
#emph[Engel's theorem] Consider the adjoint representation $\(a d\,frak(g)\)$ of a finite dimensional Lie algebra $frak(g)$, then $frak(g)$ nilpotent iff $a d\(X\)\,X in frak(g)$ can be strictly upper triangulized simultaneously iff $a d\(X\)$ is nilpotent for any $X in frak(g)$

]#label("Engel's theorem")
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $frak(g)$ be a nilpotent Lie algebra, $I lt.eq frak(g)$ is a nonzero ideal, then $I inter Z\(frak(g)\)eq.not 0$, in particular, if $I = frak(g)$ then $Z\(frak(g)\)eq.not 0$ which can also easily being shown from the fact that $Z\(frak(g)\)$ contains the last nonzero term in the lower central series of $frak(g)$

]#label("g nilpotent, I ideal => I intersect Z(g) is nontrivial")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider adjoint map restrict on $I$, since $frak(g)$ is nilpotent, $a d\(X\)$ is nilpotent for any $X in frak(g)$, so is $a d\(X\)\|_I$, i.e. $a d\(frak(g)\)_I lt.eq frak(g l)\(I\)$ is a Lie subalgebra consists of nilpotent elements, by Lemma #ref(label("Lemma for Engel's theorem"), supplement: none), there exists $0 eq.not Y in I$ such that $\[X\,Y\]= 0\,forall X in frak(g)$, thus $Y in I inter Z\(frak(g)\)$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
#emph[Lie's theorem] \ If $\(pi\,V\)$ is a finite representation of a finite dimensional Lie algebra $frak(g)$ with $overline(k) = k\,upright(c h a r) k = 0$, if $frak(g)$ is solvable, so is $pi\(frak(g)\)$, and $pi\(X\)\,X in frak(g)$ can be upper triangulized simultaneously

]#label("Lie's theorem")
#aside("Remark")[
If $\(pi\,V\)$ is a finite representation of a finite dimensional Lie algebra $frak(g)$ with $overline(k) = k\,upright(c h a r) k = 0$, if $frak(g)$ is abelian, so is $pi\(frak(g)\)$, but it doesn't imply $pi\(X\)\,X in frak(g)$ can be diagonalized simultaneously, for example $⟨mat(delim: "(", 1, 1; #none, 1)⟩ subset.eq frak(g l)\(bb(C)^2\)$ is abelian, and $⟨mat(delim: "(", 1, 1; #none, 1)⟩$ is not diagonalizable at all \ However, due to Proposition #ref(label("Finite dimensional toral Lie algebra is abelian and its elements can be diagonalized simultaneously"), supplement: none), if $\(pi\,V\)$ is a finite representation of a finite dimensional Lie algebra $frak(g)$ with $overline(k) = k$, where $frak(g)$ is abelian, so is $pi\(frak(g)\)$, and suppose $pi\(X\)\,X in frak(g)$ are diagonalizable($pi\(frak(g)\)$ is a toral Lie subalgebra), then they can be diagonalized simultaneously

]
#statement("Definition")[
A bilinear form $\(\,\)$ on Lie algebra $frak(g)$ is #emph[invariant]#index-entry("Invariant bilinear form on Lie algebra") or #emph[associative] if the Lie derivative is zero, i.e. $\(a d_Y X\,Z\)+\(X\,a d_Y Z\)= 0$, or equivalently, $\(\[X\,Y\]\,Z\)=\(X\,\[Y\,Z\]\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
A #emph[quadratic Lie algebra]#index-entry("Quadratic Lie algebra") is a Lie algebra $frak(g)$ with an invariant nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form $\(\,\): frak(g) times.o frak(g) arrow.r k$

]
#statement("Definition")[
#emph[Killing form]#index-entry("Killing form") is the bilinear map $K\(\,\): frak(g) times frak(g) arrow.r frak(g)$, $\(X\,Y\)mapsto upright(T r) (a d \( X \) a d \( Y \))$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
+ The Killing form is symmetric and invariant

+ The Killing form on a nilpotent Lie algebra is zero

+ Suppose $I lt.eq frak(g)$ is an ideal, then Killing form $K_I\(\,\)$ on $I$ is the same as the restriction of Killing form $K\(\,\)$ to $I$, i.e. $K_I\(\,\)= K\(\,\)\|_I$

]#label("Some basic properties of Killing form")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+

+ Due to Theorem #ref(label("Engel's theorem"), supplement: none)

+ By Exercise #ref(label("T:V->W<=W => Tr(T)=Tr(T|W)"), supplement: none), for $X\,Y in I$, we have #display-equation($K_I\(X\,Y\) & = T r\(a d\(X\)\|_I a d\(Y\)\|_I\)\
   & = T r (\( a d \( X \) a d \( Y \) \) \|_I)\
   & = T r\(a d\(X\)a d\(Y\)\)\
   & = K\(X\,Y\)\
   & = K\(X\,Y\)\|_I$)

]
#statement("Example")[
The Killing form is a symmetric, bilinear and invariant form, and it is nondegenerate iff $frak(g)$ is semisimple due to Proposition #ref(label("Equivalent conditions for semisimplicity"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Any invariant, symmetric and bilinear form on simple Lie algebra $frak(g)$ is a multiple of the Killing form

]#label("nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form is unique up to scalar")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Suppose $\(\,\)$ is an invariant, symmetric and bilinear form, so is $\[\,\]_c =\(\,\)- c K\(\,\)$ for any $c$. If $\(\,\)eq.not 0$, then there exists $x\,y in frak(g)$ such that $\[x\,y\]_c = 0$ for some $c$, since the kernel of $\[\,\]_c$ is a nonzero ideal, $\[\,\]_c = 0$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $frak(g)$ be a finite dimensional nonassociative $bb(F)$ algebra(including Lie algebra) with $overline(k) = k$, for any $delta in D e r\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$, let $delta = delta_s + delta_n$ be its Jordan-Chevalley decomposition in $frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$, then $delta_s\,delta_n in D e r\(frak(g)\)$

]#label("Abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition on nonassociative F-algebras")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
For any $a in k$, define $frak(g)_a$ be the generalized eigenspace of $a$, then we have $frak(g) = xor.big_(a in k) frak(g)_a$, and $\[frak(g)_a\,frak(g)_b\]subset.eq frak(g)_(a + b)$, since for any $x in frak(g)_a\,y in frak(g)_b$, $\(delta -\(a + b\)1_(frak(g))\)^m\(\[x\,y\]\)= sum_(k = 0)^m binom(m, k) [\( delta - a 1_(frak(g)) \)^(m - k) x \, \( delta - b 1_(frak(g)) \)^k y]$ which can be easily checked by induction. Then we have $delta_s\(x\)= a x\,delta_s\(y\)= b y$, and $delta_s\(\[x\,y\]\)=\(a + b\)\[x\,y\]=\[a x\,y\]+\[x\,b y\]=\[delta_s\(x\)\,y\]+\[x\,delta_s y\]$, thus $delta_s in D e r\(frak(g)\)$, so does $delta_n = delta - delta_s$

]
#statement("Definition")[
#emph[Abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition on semisimple Lie algebras] \ Because Lemma #ref(label("Abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition on nonassociative F-algebras"), supplement: none) and Proposition #ref(label("Derivations of semisimple Lie algebra are inner derivations"), supplement: none), for any $x in frak(g)$, we can identify $x$ with $a d\(x\)$, and we have Jordan-Chevalley decomposition $a d\(x\)= a d\(x\)_s + a d\(x\)_n = a d\(x_s\)+ a d\(x_n\)$, where $x_s\,x_n$ are defined to be the preimages of $a d\(x\)_s\,a d\(x\)_n$. Moreover, there exists polynomials $p\(t\)\,q\(t\)$ with no constant terms such that $a d\(x_s\)= a d\(x\)_s = p\(a d\(x\)\)$, $a d\(x_n\)= a d\(x\)_n = q\(a d\(x\)\)$, by abuse of notations, $x_s = p\(x\)$ and $x_n = q\(x\)$

]#label("Abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition on semisimple Lie algebras")
#statement("Theorem")[
Suppose $V$ is a finite dimensional $k$ vector space with $overline(k) = k$, $upright(c h a r) k = 0$, $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ is a semisimple Lie algebra, for any $x in frak(g)$, $x = x_s + x_n$ is the Jordan-Chevalley decomposition in $frak(g l)\(V\)$, moreover, the abstract and usual Jordan-Chevalley decompositions coincide, i.e. $a d\(x_s\)= a d\(x\)_s\,a d\(x_n\)= a d\(x\)_n$

]#label("Semisimple Lie algebra contains the semisimple and nilpotent parts of its elements")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Define lie subalgebras $frak(l)_W := {y in frak(g l) \( V \) mid(bar.v) y W subset.eq W \, T r \( y \|_W \) = 0}$ with $W lt.eq V$ being $frak(g)$ submodules, and define $frak(l) = (inter.big_W frak(l)_W) inter.big N_(frak(g l)\(V\))\(frak(g)\)$, for any $x in frak(g)$, due to Proposition #ref(label("T:V->W<=W => Tr(T)=Tr(T|W)"), supplement: none) and Lemma #ref(label("g semisimple, phi:g->gl(V) representation => phi(g)<=sl(V)"), supplement: none), $T r\(x\|_W\)= T r\(x\)= 0$, $frak(g) lt.eq frak(l)_W arrow.r.double frak(g) lt.eq frak(l)$, thus $frak(l)$ is a subalgebra of $N_(frak(g l)\(V\))\(frak(g)\)$ of containing $frak(g)$, thus $frak(l)$ is finite dimensional $frak(g)$ module, by Theorem #ref(label("Weyl's theorem"), supplement: none), $frak(l) = frak(g) xor frak(h)$ is a direct sum of $frak(g)$ modules, since $frak(l) lt.eq N_(frak(g l)\(V\))\(frak(g)\)$, $\[frak(g)\,frak(l)\]= 0 arrow.r.double\[frak(g)\,frak(h)\]= 0$, i.e. $frak(g)$ acts trivially on $frak(h)$, fix any irreducible $frak(g)$ submodule $W$, for any $y in frak(h)$, $x in frak(g)$, $x y - y x =\[x\,y\]= 0$, $y x v = x y v$ for $v in W$, i.e. $y in upright(H o m)_(frak(g))\(W\,W\)$, by Lemma #ref(label("Schur's lemma"), supplement: none), $y$ acts on $W$ as a scalar, but $T r\(y\|_W\)= 0$, thus $y$ acts trivially on $W$, again by Theorem #ref(label("Weyl's theorem"), supplement: none), $V$ can written as the direct sum of irreducible $frak(g)$ submodules, thus $y$ acts trivially on $W$ $arrow.r.double$ $y = 0$, therefore $frak(h)_j = 0$ $arrow.r.double$ $frak(g) = frak(l)$, for any $x in frak(g)$, due to Theorem #ref(label("Existence of Jordan-Chevalley decomposition"), supplement: none), $x = x_s + x_n$ and $x_s = p\(x\)\,x_n = q\(x\)$ for some polynomials $p\(x\)\,q\(x\)$ with no constant terms, thus if $x in frak(l)_W$, $x W subset.eq W$ and $T r\(x\|_W\)= 0$, then $x_s W = p\(x\)W subset.eq W$, $T r\(x_s\|_W\)= T r\(p\(x\|_W\)\)= 0$, similarly, $x_n W subset.eq W$, $T r\(x_n\|_W\)= 0$, $x_s\,x_n in frak(l)_W$, also $x_s = p\(x\)\,x_n = q\(x\)in N_(frak(g l)\(V\))\(frak(g)\)$, thus $x_s\,x_n in frak(l) = frak(g)$ \ Since the Jordan-Chevalley decomposition of $a d\(x\)$ in $frak(g l)\(V\)$ is unique and $a d\(x_s\)+ a d\(x_n\)= a d\(x\)= a d\(x\)_s + a d\(x\)_n$, thus $a d\(x_s\)= a d\(x\)_s\,a d\(x_n\)= a d\(x\)_n$

]
#statement("Corollary")[
Suppose $V$ is a finite dimensional $k$ vector space with $overline(k) = k$, $upright(c h a r) k = 0$, $frak(g)$ is a semisimple Lie algebra, and $phi.alt : frak(g) arrow.r frak(g l)\(V\)$ is a Lie algebra representation, $x = x_s + x_n$ is the abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition, then $phi.alt\(x\)= phi.alt\(x_s\)+ phi.alt\(x_n\)$ is the usual Jordan-Chevalley decomposition in $frak(g l)\(V\)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Due to Proposition #ref(label("image of a semisimple Lie algebra is also semisimple"), supplement: none), $phi.alt\(frak(g)\)$ is also semisimple \ First notice that a linear operator $T in upright(E n d)_k\(V\)$ is diagonalizable(semisimple) iff the dimension of the sum of its eigenspaces is $dim V$, or equivalently iff all eigenvectors span $V$ \ Since $a d\(x_s\)$ is semisimple in $frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$ as in Proposition #ref(label("Derivations of semisimple Lie algebra are inner derivations"), supplement: none), the eigenvectors $y_i$'s of $a d\(x_s\)$ spans $frak(g)$, say $a d\(x_s\)\(y_i\)= lambda_i y_i$, then as in Definition #ref(label("Adjoint representation"), supplement: none), $a d\(phi.alt\(x_s\)\)\(phi.alt\(y_i\)\)=\[phi.alt\(x_s\)\,phi.alt\(y_i\)\]= phi.alt\(\[x_s\,y_i\]\)= phi.alt\(a d\(x_s\)\(y_i\)\)= phi.alt\(lambda_i y_i\)= lambda_i phi.alt\(y_i\)$, thus those $0 eq.not phi.alt\(y_i\)$ are eigenvectors of $phi.alt\(frak(g)\)$, hence $a d\(phi.alt\(x_s\)\)$ is semisimple in $frak(g l) (frak(g l) \( V \))$ \ Since $a d\(x_n\)$ is semisimple in $frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$ as in Proposition #ref(label("Derivations of semisimple Lie algebra are inner derivations"), supplement: none), $a d\(x_n\)^m = 0$ for some $m$, then as in Definition #ref(label("Adjoint representation"), supplement: none) $a d\(phi.alt\(x_n\)\)^m = phi.alt\(a d\(x_n\)\)^m = phi.alt\(a d\(x_n\)^m\)= 0$, thus $a d\(phi.alt\(x_n\)\)$ is also nilpotent in $frak(g l) (frak(g l) \( V \))$ \ Moreover, as in Definition #ref(label("Adjoint representation"), supplement: none), $\[a d\(phi.alt\(x_s\)\)\,a d\(phi.alt\(x_n\)\)\]=\[phi.alt\(a d\(x_s\)\)\,phi.alt\(a d\(x_n\)\)\]= phi.alt\(\[a d\(x_s\)\,a d\(x_n\)\]\)= 0$ \ Thus $phi.alt\(x\)= phi.alt\(x_s\)+ phi.alt\(x_n\)$ is the abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition of $phi.alt\(x\)$ in $phi.alt\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$, by Theorem #ref(label("Semisimple Lie algebra contains the semisimple and nilpotent parts of its elements"), supplement: none), this coincide with the usual Jordan-Chevalley decomposition of $phi.alt\(x\)= phi.alt\(x\)_s + phi.alt\(x\)_n$ in $frak(g l)\(V\)$, i.e. $phi.alt\(x_s\)= phi.alt\(x\)_s$, $phi.alt\(x_n\)= phi.alt\(x\)_n$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Let $frak(g) = frak(g_1) xor dots.h.c xor frak(g_n)$ be a semisimple Lie algebra, then any ideal of $frak(g)$ is certain sum of $frak(g)_i$'s, and any sum of $frak(g)_i$'s is an ideal, in particular, $frak(g)_i$'s are ideals, moreover, $frak(g) =\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]$

]#label("g simple implies [g,g]=g")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
any ideal $I lt.eq frak(g)$ is certain sum of $frak(g)_i$'s, because $I inter frak(g)_i$ is an ideal of $frak(g)_i$ which is either $0$ or $frak(g)_i$ itself \ If $frak(g)$ is a simple Lie algebra, then it is not abelian, $\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]eq.not 0$, thus $frak(g) =\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]$, generally, $\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]=\[frak(g_1) xor dots.h.c xor frak(g_n)\,frak(g_1) xor dots.h.c xor frak(g_n)\]=\[frak(g)_1\,frak(g)_1\]xor dots.h.c xor\[frak(g)_n\,frak(g)_n\]= frak(g_1) xor dots.h.c xor frak(g_n) = frak(g)$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
If $frak(g)$ is semisimple, $phi.alt : frak(g) arrow.r frak(h)$ is a Lie algebra homomorphism, then $phi.alt\(frak(g)\)$ is also semisimple

]#label("image of a semisimple Lie algebra is also semisimple")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Suppose $frak(g) = frak(g_1) xor dots.h.c xor frak(g_n)$ is a direct sum of simple Lie algebras, $phi.alt\(frak(g)_i\)$ are also ideals so $phi.alt\(frak(g)\)= phi.alt\(frak(g)_1\)xor dots.h.c xor phi.alt\(frak(g)_n\)$ is a direct sum of ideals, and $\[phi.alt\(frak(g)_i\)\,phi.alt\(frak(g)_i\)\]= phi.alt\(\[frak(g)_i\,frak(g)_i\]\)= phi.alt\(frak(g)_i\)$ implies that each $phi.alt\(frak(g)_i\)$ is simple or $0$, thus $phi.alt\(frak(g)\)$ is also semisimple

]
#statement("Proposition")[
Let $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ be a Lie algebra, $X in frak(g)$, then $X$ is nilpotent $arrow.r.double$ $a d\(X\)$ is nilpotent, if in addition $V$ is finite dimensional and $overline(k) = k$, then $X$ is semisimple $arrow.r.double$ $a d\(X\)$ is semisimple, or rather diagonalizable

]#label("nilpotent/semisimple implies ad-nilpotent/ad-semisimple")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $L_X\,R_X$ be left and right multiplications by $X$, then we have $a d_X = L_X - R_X$ and $\[L_X\,R_X\]= 0$, thus $X$ is nilpotent $arrow.r.double$ $a d\(X\)$ is nilpotent \ Notice that given $A =\(a_(i j)\)$, $D = upright(d i a g)\(d_1\,dots.h.c\,d_n\)$, $\[D\,A\]=\(\(d_i - d_j\)a_(i j)\)$, thus $\[D\,E_(i j)\]=\(d_i - d_j\)E_(i j)$, thus $X$ is diagonalizble $arrow.r.double$ $a d\(X\)$ is diagonalizable

]
#statement("Theorem", title: [Cartan's criterion for solvability])[
#index-entry("Cartan’s criterion for solvability") Let $V$ be a finite dimensional $k$ vector space with $"char" k = 0$, $frak(g) lt.eq frak(g l)\(V\)$ is a Lie subalgebra, then $frak(g)$ is solvable iff $"Tr"\(X Y\)= 0$, $forall X in frak(g)\,Y in\[frak(g)\,frak(g)\]$

]#label("Cartan's criterion for solvability")
#statement("Corollary")[
#emph[Cartan's criterion for semisimplicity]#index-entry("Cartan’s criterion for semisimplicity") \ Let $frak(g)$ is finite dimensional Lie algebra with $"char" k = 0$, then $frak(g)$ is semisimple iff its Killing form is nondegenerate

]#label("Cartan's criterion for semisimplicity")
#statement("Proposition")[
The following statements are equivalent

#emph[\(a)] $frak(g)$ is semisimple

#emph[\(b)] The Killing form is nondegenerate

#emph[\(c)] $frak(g)$ doesn't have have nontrivial abelian ideals

#emph[\(d)] $frak(g)$ doesn't have have nontrivial solvable ideals

#emph[\(e)] $"rad"\(frak(g)\)= 0$

]#label("Equivalent conditions for semisimplicity")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$\(a\)arrow.l.r.double\(b\)$ is due to Corollary #ref(label("Cartan's criterion for semisimplicity"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Example")[
$quad$ Recall $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)= {X in M \( 2 \, k \) mid(bar.v) "Tr" \( X \) = 0} = {mat(delim: "(", a, b; c, - a) mid(bar.v) a \, b \, c in k}$ \ $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)= chevron.l H\,X\,Y chevron.r$, where $X = mat(delim: "(", 0, 1; 0, 0)\,Y = mat(delim: "(", 0, 0; 1, 0)\,H = mat(delim: "(", 1, 0; 0, - 1)$, and $a d_H X =\[H\,X\]= 2 X\,a d_H Y =\[H\,Y\]= - 2 Y\,a d_X Y =\[X\,Y\]= H$, this is the adjoint representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$

]#label("Adjoint representation of SL(2,F)")
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $\(pi\,V\)$ be a finite dimensional representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$, if $V = k^n$, then there are theree $n times n$ matrices $x\,y\,h$ such that $\[h\,x\]= 2 x\,\[h\,y\]= - 2 y\,\[x\,y\]= h$ due to Example #ref(label("Adjoint representation of SL(2,F)"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $\(pi\,V\)$ be a finite dimensional representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$, $V_lambda := {v in V mid(bar.v) pi \( H \) v = lambda v}$, then $pi\(X\)V_lambda subset.eq V_(lambda + 2)$, $pi\(Y\)V_lambda subset.eq V_(lambda - 2)$ and $pi\(H\)V_lambda subset.eq V_lambda$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$pi\(H\)V_lambda subset.eq V_lambda$ is just by definition, suppose $v in V_lambda$, $pi\(H\)pi\(X\)v = 2 pi\(X\)v + pi\(X\)pi\(H\)v =\(lambda + 2\)pi\(X\)v$, $pi\(H\)pi\(Y\)v = - 2 pi\(Y\)v + pi\(Y\)pi\(H\)v =\(lambda - 2\)pi\(Y\)v$,

]
#aside("Remark")[
$pi\(X\)\,pi\(Y\)$ are named #emph[raising and lowering operator]

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Suppose $overline(k) = k\,"char" k = 0$, for any integer $m gt.eq 0$, there is an irreducible representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$ with dimension $m + 1$

]#label("Classification of representations of sl(2,F)")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $\(pi\,V\)$ be a finite dimensional irreducible representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$, there exists highest $lambda in k$ such that $V_lambda eq.not 0$, pick $0 eq.not u in V_lambda$, let $u_k := pi\(Y\)^k u in V_(lambda - 2 k)$, then there exists $m$ such that $u_m eq.not 0$ but $u_(m + 1) = 0$, then $u_0\,dots.h.c\,u_m$ are independent since they belong distinct eigenspaces, with $pi\(H\)u_k =\(lambda - 2 k\)u_k$, $pi\(X\)u_0 = pi\(X\)u = 0$ since $u in V_lambda$ is of \"highest weight\", and $pi\(X\)u_k = k\(lambda - k + 1\)u_(k - 1)\,k > 0$ by induction, $pi\(X\)u_1 = pi\(X\)pi\(Y\)u =\(\[pi\(X\)\,pi\(Y\)\)\]+ pi\(Y\)pi\(X\)\)u = pi\(H\)u + pi\(Y\)pi\(X\)u = lambda u = 1\(lambda - 1 + 1\)u_0$, $pi\(X\)u_(k + 1) = pi\(X\)pi\(Y\)u_k =\(\[pi\(X\)\,pi\(Y\)\)\]+ pi\(Y\)pi\(X\)\)u_k = pi\(H\)u_k + pi\(Y\)pi\(X\)u_k =\(lambda - 2 k\)u_k + k\(lambda - k + 1\)pi\(Y\)u_(k - 1) =\(k + 1\)\(lambda - k\)u_k$ \ Note that since $0 = X u_(m + 1) =\(m + 1\)\(lambda - m\)u_m arrow.r.double lambda = m$, which implies all possible eigenvalue for $pi\(H\)$ has to be integers, when $m$ is even, we call this irreducible representation even, when $m$ is odd, we call this irreducible representation odd \ In general, for any finite dimensional representation, we can decompose the representation into irreducible subrepresentations by using this procedure repeatedly \ Therefore $0 eq.not W := chevron.l u_0\,dots.h.c\,u_m chevron.r$ is invariant, but $pi$ is irreducible, thus $V = W$, and by Lemma #ref(label("Schur's lemma"), supplement: none), $\(pi\,V\)$ is unique up to isomorphism

]
#statement("Example")[
$\(a d\,frak(s l)\(2\,k\)\)$ is the unique irreducible $3$ dimensional representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$ with $V_0 = chevron.l H chevron.r$, $V_(- 2) = chevron.l Y chevron.r$ and $V_2 = chevron.l X chevron.r$, it is irreducible because of Lemma #ref(label("Count the number irreducible summand of a representation of sl(2,F)"), supplement: none), if we use $X\,Y\,H$ as basis, then $a d\(X\)\,a d\(Y\)\,a d\(H\)$ would have the matrix forms #display-equation($mat(delim: "(", 0, 0, - 2; 0, 0, 0; 0, 1, 0)\,mat(delim: "(", 0, 0, 0; 0, 0, 2; - 1, 0, 0)\,mat(delim: "(", 2, 0, 0; 0, - 2, 0; 0, 0, 0)$) Thus #display-equation($K\(X\,X\)= T r mat(delim: "(", 0, - 2, 0; 0, 0, 0; 0, 0, 0) = 0\,quad K\(X\,Y\)= T r mat(delim: "(", 2, 0, 0; 0, 0, 0; 0, 0, 2) = 4$)#display-equation($K\(X\,H\)= T r mat(delim: "(", 0, 0, 0; 0, 0, 0; 0, - 2, 0) = 0\,quad K\(Y\,Y\)= T r mat(delim: "(", 0, 0, 0; - 2, 0, 0; 0, 0, 0) = 0$)#display-equation($K\(Y\,H\)= T r mat(delim: "(", 0, 0, 0; 0, 0, 0; - 2, 0, 0) = 0\,quad K\(H\,H\)= T r mat(delim: "(", 4, 0, 0; 0, 4, 0; 0, 0, 0) = 8$)Thus its Cartan matrix is $Phi = mat(delim: "(", 0, 4, 0; 4, 0, 0; 0, 0, 8)$ which is nondegenerate

]#label("Adjoint representation of sl(2,F) is the unique 3 dimensional irreducible representation")
#statement("Example")[
The tautological representation $\(tau\,k^2\)$ is the unique irreducible $2$ dimensional representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$ with $V_1 = ⟨vec(1, 0)⟩$, $V_(- 1) = ⟨vec(0, 1)⟩$

]#label("Tautological representation of sl(2,F)")
#statement("Example")[
Let $S^k (k^2)$ be the $k$-th symmetric power of $k^2$ which is isomorphic to the the set of degree $k$ polynomials in $k\[x\,y\]$ generated by \ $⟨x^k \, x^(k - 1) y \, dots.h.c \, x y^(k - 1) \, y^k⟩$ which is of dimension $k + 1$, with this identification, $(pi \, S^k (k^2))$ with $pi\(X\)\(x\)= mat(delim: "(", 0, 1; 0, 0) vec(1, 0) = vec(0, 0) = 0$, $pi\(X\)y = x$, $pi\(Y\)x = y$, $pi\(Y\)y = 0$, $pi\(H\)x = x$, $pi\(H\)y = - y$, just as in Example #ref(label("Tautological representation of sl(2,F)"), supplement: none), and define inductively that $pi\(Z\)\(f g\)= g pi\(Z\)f + f pi\(Z\)g$, this is the unique $k + 1$ dimensional irreducible representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Let $\(pi\,V\)$ be a finite dimensional irreducible representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$, and $V_k$ be the $k$-eigenspace of $pi\(H\)$, then the number of irreducible summand of $\(pi\,V\)$ is $dim V_0 + dim V_1$, whereas $dim V_0\,dim V_1$ are number of even and odd irreducible summands

]#label("Count the number irreducible summand of a representation of sl(2,F)")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
In an even irreducible representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$ all the eigenvalues are even, so there is a unique $0$-eigenvector, in an odd irreducible representation of $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$ all the eigenvalues are odd, so there is a unique $1$-eigenvector, thus the number of irreducible summand of $\(pi\,V\)$ is $dim V_0 + dim V_1$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$frak(g)$ is a semisimple Lie algebra, $frak(h)$ is a maximal toral Lie algebra. For $alpha in frak(h)^(*) = upright(H o m)_(bb(F))\(frak(h)\,k\)$, define #emph[root spaces]#index-entry("Root space") #display-equation($frak(g)_alpha = {x in frak(g) mid(bar.v) a d_h \( x \) = \[ h \, x \] = alpha \( h \) x \, forall h in frak(h)}$) $alpha$ is a #emph[root]#index-entry("Root") if $alpha eq.not 0$ and $frak(g)_alpha eq.not 0$, denote the set of roots as $Delta$. $frak(g)_0 = C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ is the centralizer of $frak(h)$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
+ $\[frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_beta\]subset.eq frak(g)_(alpha + beta)$

+ $alpha in Delta$, any $X in frak(g)_alpha$ is nilpotent

+ $K\(frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_beta\)= 0$ unless $alpha + beta = 0$

+ $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(g)_0)$ is nondegenerate

]#label("Basic properties of root spaces")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+ For $X in frak(g)_alpha$, $Y in frak(g)_beta$, $Z in frak(h)$, we have #display-equation($\[Z\,\[X\,Y\]\]=\[\[Z\,X\]\,Y\]+\[X\,\[Z\,Y\]\]= alpha\(Z\)\[X\,Y\]+ beta\(Z\)\[X\,Y\]=\(alpha + beta\)\(Z\)\[X\,Y\]$)

+ For $beta in Delta union { 0 }$, $alpha in Delta$, $X in frak(g)_alpha\,Y in frak(g)_beta$, $a d\(X\)^n\(Y\)in frak(g)_(n alpha + beta) = 0$ when $n$ is big enough, thus $a d\(X\)$ is nilpotent

+ Supppose $alpha + beta eq.not 0$, then there exists $Z in frak(h)$ such that $\(alpha + beta\)\(Z\)eq.not 0$, then for any $X in frak(g)_alpha\,Y in frak(g)_beta$ #display-equation($\(alpha + beta\)\(Z\)K\(X\,Y\) & = alpha\(Z\)K\(X\,Y\)+ beta\(Z\)K\(X\,Y\)\
   & = K\(alpha\(Z\)X\,Y\)+ K\(X\,beta\(Z\)Y\)\
   & = K\(\[Z\,X\]\,Y\)+ K\(X\,\[Z\,Y\]\)\
   & = - K\(\[X\,Z\]\,Y\)+ K\(X\,\[Z\,Y\]\)\
   & = 0$) Thus $K\(X\,Y\)= 0$

+ Since $K\(frak(g)_alpha\,frak(h)\)= 0\,forall alpha in Delta$, $ker K\(\,\)\|_(frak(g)_0) subset.eq ker K\(\,\)= 0$, thus $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(g)_0)$ is nondegenerate

]
#statement("Theorem")[
Semisimple Lie algebra $frak(g)$ has root space decomposition $frak(g) = xor.big_(alpha in Delta union { 0 }) frak(g)_alpha$

]#label("Root space decomposition")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
By Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$V$ is an $k$ vector space, $x\,y in frak(g l)\(V\)$ commutes, $x$ is nilpotent, then $x y$ is nilpotent, and $T r\(x y\)= 0$

]#label("x,y in gl(V) commutes, x nilpotent => xy nilpotent")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$x^m = 0 arrow.r.double\(x y\)^m = x^m y^m = 0$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
For semisimple Lie algebra $frak(g)$ with maximal toral Lie subalgebra $frak(h)$, $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)= frak(h)$

]#label("Maximal toral Lie algebra of semisimple Lie algebra is self centralizing")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+ $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ contains semisimple and nilpotent parts of its elements

  If $x in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$, due to Proposition #ref(label("Abstract Jordan-Chevalley decomposition on semisimple Lie algebras"), supplement: none), there are polynomials $p\(t\)\,q\(t\)$ with no constant terms such that $a d\(x_s\)= a d\(x\)_s = p\(a d\(x\)\)\,a d\(x_n\)= a d\(x\)_n = q\(a d\(x\)\)$, since $x in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$, $a d\(x\)\|_(frak(h)) = 0$, , $a d\(x_s\)\|_(frak(h)) = p\(a d\(x\)\)\|_(frak(h)) = 0$, $a d\(x_n\)\|_(frak(h)) = q\(a d\(x\)\)\|_(frak(h)) = 0$, thus $x_s\,x_n in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$

+ $frak(h)$ contains all semisimple elements of $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$

  If $s in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ be a semisimple element, use Exercise #ref(label("Pairwise commuting matrices can be diagonalized simultaneously"), supplement: none), $s$ and elements of $frak(h)$ are diagonalizable simultaneously, thus $frak(h) + chevron.l s chevron.r$ is toral in $frak(g)$, then $s in frak(h)$ since $frak(h)$ is maximal

+ $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$ is nondegenerate

  Suppose there exists $h in frak(h)$ such that $K\(h\,frak(h)\)= 0$, if $n in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ be a nilpotent element, then $a d\(n\)$ is nilpotent, and $\[n\,frak(h)\]= 0$, thus $\[a d\(n\)\,a d\(h\)\]= a d\(\[n\,h\]\)= 0$, by Lemma #ref(label("x,y in gl(V) commutes, x nilpotent => xy nilpotent"), supplement: none), $T r\(a d\(n\)a d\(h\)\)= 0$, if $s in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ be a semisimple element, according to Step II, $s in frak(h)$, thus $K\(s\,h\)= 0$, and according to Step I, $K\(h\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\)= 0$ which contradicts Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(d) that $K\(\,\)\|_(C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\))$ is nondegenerate

+ $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ is nilpotent

  If $n in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ be a nilpotent element, then $a d\(n\)$ is nilpotent, so is $a d\(n\)\|_(C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\))$, if $s in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ be a semisimple element, according to Step II, $s in frak(h)$, $a d\(s\)\|_(C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)) = 0$, by Theorem #ref(label("Engel's theorem"), supplement: none), $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ is nilpotent

+ $frak(h) inter\[C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]= 0$

  Suppose $x in frak(h) inter\[C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]$, then $x = sum\[y_i\,z_i\]$ where $y_i\,z_i in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$, then $K\(x\,x\)= sum K\(x\,\[y_i\,z_i\]\)= sum K\(\[x\,y_i\]\,z_i\)= 0$, since $K\(\,\)$ is nondegenerate on $frak(h)$\(or $frak(g)$ or $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$), thus $x = 0$

+ $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ is abelian

  Suppose $\[C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]eq.not 0$, since $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ is nilpotent from Step IV, by Lemma #ref(label("g nilpotent, I ideal => I intersect Z(g) is nontrivial"), supplement: none), there exists $0 eq.not z in Z\(C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\)inter\[C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]$, then $z$ can't be semisimple, otherwise $z in frak(h) inter\[C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]$, contradicting Step V, thus its nilpotent part $n eq.not 0$, but its semisimple part $s in frak(h) lt.eq Z\(C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\)$, so is $n = z - s$, but then $\[n\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]= 0$, by Lemma #ref(label("x,y in gl(V) commutes, x nilpotent => xy nilpotent"), supplement: none), $K\(n\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\)= 0$, contradicting Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(d)

+ $frak(h) = C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$

  Suppose $x in C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\\frak(h)$, then it gives a nonzero nilpotent part $n$, but then since $C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)$ is abelian by Step VI, thus $\[n\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\]= 0$, by Lemma #ref(label("x,y in gl(V) commutes, x nilpotent => xy nilpotent"), supplement: none), $K\(n\,C_(frak(g))\(frak(h)\)\)= 0$, contradicting Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(d)

]
#aside("Remark")[
$K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$ is nondegenerate is not the same as saying that the Killing form of $frak(h)$ is nondegenerate which obviously violates Proposition #ref(label("Equivalent conditions for semisimplicity"), supplement: none), it doesn't contradict Proposition #ref(label("Some basic properties of Killing form"), supplement: none) since $frak(h) lt.eq frak(g)$ is merely a Lie subalgebra but not an ideal, by the nondegeneracy, we can identity $frak(h)^(*)$ with $frak(h)$ by $frak(h)^(*) arrow.r frak(h)\,alpha mapsto t_alpha$, where $K\(t_alpha\,x\)= alpha\(x\)$, and here $t$ behaves like the linear isomorphism $t : frak(h)^(*) arrow.r frak(h)\,alpha mapsto t_alpha$

]
#statement("Proposition")[
+ $Delta$ spans $frak(h)^(*)$

+ $alpha in Delta arrow.r.double - alpha in Delta$

+ $alpha in Delta$, $x in frak(g)_alpha$, $y in frak(g)_(- alpha)$, then $\[x\,y\]= K\(x\,y\)t_alpha$

+ $alpha in Delta$, then $0 eq.not\[frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_(- alpha)\]= chevron.l t_alpha chevron.r$

+ If $alpha in Delta$, then $alpha\(t_alpha\)= K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\)eq.not 0$

+ If $alpha in Delta$, $0 eq.not x_alpha in frak(g)_alpha$, then there exists $y_alpha in frak(g)_(- alpha)$ such that $K\(x_alpha\,y_alpha\)= frac(2, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\))$, define $h_alpha :=\[x_alpha\,y_alpha\]= frac(2 t_alpha, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\))$, then $frak(s)_alpha := chevron.l x_alpha\,y_alpha\,h_alpha chevron.r$ is isomorphic to $frak(s l)\(2\,k\)$ via $x_alpha mapsto mat(delim: "(", 0, 1; 0, 0)\,y_alpha mapsto mat(delim: "(", 0, 0; 1, 0)\,h_alpha mapsto mat(delim: "(", 1, 0; 0, - 1)$

+ Given $alpha in Delta$, $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(g)\)$ will be a representation of $frak(s)_alpha$, thus $frak(g)$ can be decomposed into irreducible representations of $frak(s)_alpha$, and the highest eigenvectors for this representation are also common highest eigenvectors of $a d\(frak(h)\)$

]#label("Some properties about root space decomposition")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
+ Suppose $chevron.l Delta chevron.r subset.neq frak(h)^(*)$, then there exists $0 eq.not h in frak(h)$, such that $forall alpha in Delta\,alpha\(h\)= 0$, then $forall x in frak(g)_alpha\,\[h\,x\]= alpha\(h\)x = 0$, and since $frak(h)$ is abelian, $\[h\,frak(h)\]= 0$, thus $\[h\,frak(g)\]= 0$, but then $h in Z\(frak(g)\)= 0$ which is a contradiction

+ If $alpha in Delta$, and $frak(g)_(- alpha) = 0$, then $K\(frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_beta\)= 0\,forall beta$ by Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(c), then $frak(g)_alpha = 0$ which is a contradiction

+ $forall h in frak(h)\,K\(h\,\[x\,y\]\)= K\(\[h\,x\]\,y\)= K\(alpha\(h\)x\,y\)= K\(t_alpha\,h\)K\(x\,y\)= K\(h\,K\(x\,y\)t_alpha\)$, since $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$ is nondegenerate, $\[x\,y\]= K\(x\,y\)t_alpha$

+ Only need to show that $\[frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_(- alpha)\]eq.not 0$. There exists $x in frak(g)_alpha\,y in frak(g)_(- alpha)$ such that $K\(x\,y\)eq.not 0$, otherwise then $K\(frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_beta\)= 0\,forall beta$ by Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(c), then $frak(g)_alpha = 0$ which is a contradiction, thus $\[x\,y\]= K\(x\,y\)t_alpha eq.not 0$ by (c)

+ Suppose instead $alpha\(t_alpha\)= 0$, then $\[t_alpha\,x\]=\[t_alpha\,y\]= 0\,forall x in frak(g)_alpha\,y in frak(g)_(- alpha)$, by nondegeneracy, we can find $x in frak(g)_alpha\,y in frak(g)_(- alpha)$ such that $K\(x\,y\)= 1$, then by (c), $\[x\,y\]= K\(x\,y\)t_alpha = t_alpha$, thus $frak(s) = chevron.l x\,y\,t_alpha chevron.r tilde.equiv a d\(frak(s)\)lt.eq a d\(frak(g)\)lt.eq frak(g l)\(frak(g)\)$ is a $3$ dimensional solvable Lie algebra, by Theorem #ref(label("Lie's theorem"), supplement: none), for any $s in\[frak(s)\,frak(s)\]$, ad(s) is nilpotent, thus $a d\(t_alpha\)$ is both semisimple and nilpotent, hence $a d\(t_alpha\)= 0 arrow.r.double t_alpha = 0$ which is a contradiction

+ $\[h_alpha\,x_alpha\]= frac(2, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\))\[t_alpha\,x_alpha\]= frac(2, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\)) alpha\(t_alpha\)x_alpha = 2 x_alpha$ $\[h_alpha\,y_alpha\]= frac(2, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\))\[t_alpha\,y_alpha\]= - frac(2, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\)) alpha\(t_alpha\)y_alpha = - 2 y_alpha$

+ Suppose $x in frak(g)$ is a highest eigenvector of representation $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(g)\)$, then $0 = a d\(x_alpha\)\(x\)=\[x_alpha\,x\]$, $forall h in frak(h)\,\[x_alpha\,a d\(h\)\(x\)\]=\[x_alpha\,\[h\,x\]\]=\[\[x_alpha\,h\]\,x\]+\[h\,\[x_alpha\,x\]\]=\[- alpha\(h\)x_alpha\,x\]= 0$

]
#aside("Remark")[
In (f), the choice of $x_alpha$ is not canonical, however, $h_alpha = frac(2 t_alpha, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\))$ is canonical, for example, if we pick $0 eq.not x_(- alpha) in frak(g)_(- alpha)$, $frak(s)_(- alpha) = chevron.l x_(- alpha)\,y_(- alpha)\,h_(- alpha) chevron.r$, then $h_(- alpha) = h_alpha = frac(2 t_(- alpha), K\(t_(- alpha)\,t_(- alpha)\)) = - h_alpha$, moreover, according to Lemma #ref(label("nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form is unique up to scalar"), supplement: none), any nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form on $frak(h)$ is of the form $\(\,\):= c K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$ for some $c eq.not 0$, then $t'_alpha$ the dual of $alpha in frak(h)^(*)$ given by $\(t'_alpha\,x\)= alpha\(x\)\,forall x in frak(h)$, then we have $K\(t_alpha\,x\)= alpha\(x\)=\(t'_alpha\,x\)= c K\(t'_alpha\,x\)= K\(c t'_alpha\,x\)$, because of the nondegeneracy of $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$, $t_alpha = c t'_alpha arrow.r.double t'_alpha = t_alpha / c$, and $frac(2 t'_alpha, \(t'_alpha\,t'_alpha\)) = frac(2 t_alpha / c, c K (t_alpha / c \, t_alpha / c)) = frac(2 t_alpha, K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\)) = h_alpha$, thus $h_alpha$ is even canonical defined regardless of the choice of the nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form on $frak(h)$, for this reason, we give $h_alpha$ a new notation $alpha^or$ for later use, whereas $alpha\(alpha^or\)= 2$, for any nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form on $frak(h)$, note that $alpha mapsto alpha^or$ is not linear \ Also, according to Lemma #ref(label("nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form is unique up to scalar"), supplement: none), even though $\(\,\)$ is defined up to a scalar, but the orthogonality is always well defined \ Due to Proposition #ref(label("Some properties about root space decomposition"), supplement: none)\(g), if $0 eq.not x in frak(g)$ is a highest eigenvector for representation of $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(g)\)$, then $x in frak(g)_beta$, for some $beta in Delta union { 0 }$, when $beta = 0$, these corresponds to the trivial representations, if $beta in Delta$, we can denote one such highest eigenvector $0 eq.not x_beta in frak(g)_beta$, then $a d\(h_alpha\)\(x_beta\)=\[h_alpha\,x_beta\]= beta\(h_alpha\)x_beta$, thus $x_beta$ is a $beta\(h_alpha\)= frac(2 K\(t_alpha\,t_beta\), K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\))$-eigenvector, by Proposition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(a), $a d\(y_alpha\)^j\(x_beta\)in frak(g)_(beta - j alpha)$ are all the nonzero eigenvectors corresponds to eigenvalues $\(beta - j alpha\)\(h_alpha\)= 2 (frac(K\(t_alpha\,t_beta\), K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\)) - j)$, and these roots $beta - j alpha\,j = 0\,dots.h.c\,k = beta\(h_alpha\)$ form an $alpha$-string \ One of these irreducible representation is $frak(s)_alpha$ itself according to Example #ref(label("Adjoint representation of sl(2,F) is the unique 3 dimensional irreducible representation"), supplement: none)

]#label("alpha check is canonical")
#statement("Example")[
Consider $frak(g) = frak(frak(s l))\(2\,bb(C)\)$ which is a semisimple Lie algebra, then #display-equation($frak(h) = {mat(delim: "(", z_1; #none, dots.down; #none, , z_n) mid(bar.v) sum z_i = 0}$) is a maximal toral Lie subalgebra, denote $upright(d i a g)\(z_1\,dots.h.c\,z_n\)$ as $h_z$, then $\[h_z\,E_(i j)\]=\(z_i - z_j\)E_(i j)$, and $frak(frak(s l))\(2\,bb(C)\)= frak(g) = frak(h) xor.big_(i eq.not j) chevron.l E_(i j) chevron.r$, $Delta = { e_i - e_j\|i eq.not j }$, where $e_i in frak(h)^(*)$ is defined by $e_i\(h_z\)= z_i$, also #display-equation($frak(s)_alpha = {mat(delim: "(", 0; #none, dots.down; #none, , a, , b; #none, , , dots.down; #none, , b, , - a; #none, , , , , , dots.down; #none, , , , , , , 0; #none)} tilde.equiv frak(s l)\(2\,bb(C)\)$) where $alpha = e_i - e_j$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Define $s_alpha\(beta\):= beta - frac(2 K\(t_alpha\,t_beta\), K\(t_alpha\,t_alpha\)) alpha\,forall alpha in Delta\,beta in frak(h)^(*)$ is an orthogonal reflection over the hyperplane $H_alpha = {alpha \( x \) = K \( t_alpha \, x \) = 0 mid(bar.v) x in frak(h)} = ker alpha$, more precisely, $s_alpha\(alpha\)= - alpha$, $s_alpha\(beta\)= beta\,forall beta in H_alpha$, note here any nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form $\(\,\)$ can be used as the definition in place of the Killing form $K\(\,\)$ thanks to Lemma #ref(label("nondegenerate, symmetric, bilinear and invariant form is unique up to scalar"), supplement: none)

]#label("Definition of s_alpha")
#statement("Proposition")[
$quad$ #emph[\(a)] If $alpha in Delta$, then $dim frak(g)_alpha = 1$ \ #emph[\(b)] If $alpha in Delta$, then $c alpha in Delta arrow.l.r.double c = plus.minus 1$ \ #emph[\(c)] If $alpha\,beta\,alpha + beta in Delta$, then $\[frak(g)_alpha\,frak(g)_beta\]= frak(g)_(alpha + beta)$ \ #emph[\(d)] If $alpha\,beta in Delta$, the Cartan integer $beta\(h_alpha\)= frac(2 K\(alpha\,beta\), K\(alpha\,alpha\)) in bb(Z)$ and $beta - beta\(h_alpha\)alpha in Delta$, if $beta eq.not alpha\,- alpha$, then $Delta inter.big {beta + j alpha mid(bar.v) j in bb(Z)} = {beta + j alpha mid(bar.v) - r lt.eq j lt.eq s \, j in bb(Z)}$ which is an $alpha$ string through $beta$, and $beta\(h_alpha\)= r - s$

]#label("alpha string through beta")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
#emph[\(a)] Let $frak(m) = frak(h) xor.big_(c alpha in Delta\,c in k^times) frak(g)_(c alpha)$, then $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(m)\)$ is a finite representation of $frak(s)_alpha$ because of Proppsition #ref(label("Basic properties of root spaces"), supplement: none)\(a), first notice for $x in frak(g)_(c alpha)$, we have $a d\(h_alpha\)\(x\)=\[h_alpha\,x\]= c alpha\(h_alpha\)x = 2 c x$, thus the $0$-eigenspace of $a d\(h_alpha\)$ is $frak(h)$, but note that for any $h in ker alpha = H_alpha lt.eq frak(h)$ as in Definition #ref(label("Definition of s_alpha"), supplement: none), $a d\(x_alpha\)\(h\)=\[x_alpha\,h\]= alpha\(h\)x_alpha = 0$, $a d\(y_alpha\)\(h\)=\[y_alpha\,h\]= - alpha\(h\)y_alpha = 0$, $a d\(h_alpha\)\(h\)=\[h_alpha\,h\]= 0$, thus $frak(s)_alpha$ acts trivially on $ker alpha$ which is of codimension $1$ in $frak(h)$ which gives $dim frak(h) - 1$ copies of trivial representation of $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(m)\)$, since $h_alpha in.not ker alpha$, $frak(h) = chevron.l h_alpha chevron.r xor ker alpha$, by Example #ref(label("Adjoint representation of sl(2,F) is the unique 3 dimensional irreducible representation"), supplement: none) and Lemma #ref(label("Count the number irreducible summand of a representation of sl(2,F)"), supplement: none), $frak(s)_alpha = chevron.l x_alpha\,y_alpha\,h_alpha chevron.r$ is a $3$ dimensional irreducible representation of $frak(s)_alpha$, and $frak(s)_alpha xor ker alpha$ are the only possible even irreducible representations of $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(m)\)$, thus $dim frak(g)_alpha = 1$, otherwise, we can choose $0 eq.not x'_alpha in frak(g)_alpha$ linearly independent of $x_alpha$, then we have $\[h_alpha\,x'_alpha\]= alpha\(h_alpha\)x'_alpha = 2 x'_alpha$, which gives a contradiction. Moreover, $2 alpha in.not Delta$, otherwise, we can choose $0 eq.not x_(2 alpha) in frak(g)_(2 alpha)$, then $\[h_alpha\,x_(2 alpha)\]= 2 alpha\(h_alpha\)x_(2 alpha) = 4 x_(2 alpha)$which also gives a contradiction \ #emph[\(b)] Suppose $c alpha in Delta$, for $0 eq.not x in frak(g)_(c alpha)$, we have $a d\(h_alpha\)\(x\)=\[h_alpha\,x\]= c alpha\(h_alpha\)x = 2 c x$, by Theorem #ref(label("Classification of representations of sl(2,F)"), supplement: none), we know that $2 c in bb(Z)$, but by symmetry, if we let $beta = c alpha$, then $alpha = beta / c in Delta$ implies $2 / c in bb(Z)$, thus $c$ can only possibly be $plus.minus 1\,plus.minus 2\,plus.minus 1 / 2$, but from (a), we know $c eq.not 2$, thus $c eq.not - 2$ thanks to Proposition #ref(label("Some properties about root space decomposition"), supplement: none)\(b), and by symmetry, $c eq.not plus.minus 1 / 2$, therefore $frak(m) = frak(h) xor frak(g)_alpha xor frak(g)_(- alpha) = ker alpha xor chevron.l h_alpha chevron.r xor frak(g)_alpha xor frak(g)_(- alpha) = ker alpha xor frak(s)_alpha$ \ #emph[\(c)] Obiviously $beta$ can't be $alpha$ or $alpha$, otherwise $alpha + beta in.not Delta$, also $beta + j alpha eq.not 0\,forall j in k$ by (b), for $beta in Delta\\{ alpha\,- alpha }$, we can consider $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(m)\)$ which is a finite representation of $frak(s)_alpha$ with $frak(m) = xor.big_(beta + j alpha in Delta\,j in bb(Z)) frak(g)_(beta + j alpha)$, suppose $beta + j alpha in Delta$, $dim frak(g)_(beta + j alpha) = 1$ by (a), choose $0 eq.not x_(beta + j alpha) in frak(g)_(beta + j alpha)$, we have $\[h_alpha\,x_(beta + j alpha)\]=\(beta + j alpha\)\(h_alpha\)x_(beta + j alpha) = (beta \( h_alpha \) + 2 j) x_(beta + j alpha)$, as $j$ varies in $bb(Z)$, $beta\(h_alpha\)+ 2 j$ can't take both $0$ and $1$, thus the sum of dimension of $0$-eigenspace and $1$-eigenspace of $a d\(h_alpha\)$ on $frak(m)$ is $1$, by Lemma #ref(label("Count the number irreducible summand of a representation of sl(2,F)"), supplement: none), $\(a d\|_(frak(s)_alpha)\,frak(m)\)$ is irreducible, according to Theorem #ref(label("Classification of representations of sl(2,F)"), supplement: none) and Remark #ref(label("alpha check is c