#proof(title: "Proof")[
Since $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$ is nondegenerate, we define the dual of $K\(\,\)\|_(frak(h))$ on $frak(h)^(*)$ as $\(alpha\,beta\):= K\(t_alpha\,t_beta\)$, which is also nondegenerate \ #emph[\(1)] Since $frak(h)^(*) = k Delta$ by Proposition #ref(label("Some properties about root space decomposition"), supplement: none)\(a), Pick any basis in $Delta$, say $alpha_1\,dots.h.c\,alpha_m$, then $forall beta in Delta$, $beta = sum c_j alpha_j\,c_j in k$, we have $\(beta\,alpha_k\)= sum c_j\(alpha_j\,alpha_k\)arrow.r.double frac(2\(beta\,alpha_k\), \(alpha_k\,alpha_k\)) = sum c_j frac(2\(alpha_j\,alpha_k\), \(alpha_k\,alpha_k\))$ whereas $frac(2\(beta\,alpha_k\), \(alpha_k\,alpha_k\))\,frac(2\(alpha_j\,alpha_k\), \(alpha_k\,alpha_k\))$ are all integers by Proposition #ref(label("alpha string through beta"), supplement: none)\(d), since $(beta - sum c_j alpha_j \, alpha_k) = 0$ and that $\(\,\)$ is nondegenerate, meaning $c_j$'s are the unique solution, hence matrix $(\( alpha_j \, alpha_k \))$ is nonsingular, so is matrix $(frac(2\(alpha_j\,alpha_k\), \(alpha_k\,alpha_k\)))$, thus $c_j in bb(Q)$, which means $dim_(bb(Q)) V = dim_k frak(h)^(*)$ and $frak(h)^(*) = V times.o_(bb(Q)) k$ \ #emph[\(2)] For $0 eq.not x_alpha in frak(g)_alpha$, $a d\(h\)a d\(k\)\(x_alpha\)=\[h\,\[k\,x_alpha\]\]= alpha\(k\)\[h\,x_alpha\]= alpha\(h\)alpha\(k\)x_alpha$, according to Theorem #ref(label("Root space decomposition"), supplement: none), we have #display-equation($K\(h\,k\)= T r\(a d\(h\)a d\(k\)\)= 0 + sum_(alpha in Delta) T r (a d \( h \) \|_(frak(g)_alpha) a d \( k \) \|_(frak(g)_alpha)) = sum_(alpha in Delta) alpha\(h\)alpha\(k\)$)Due to Proposition #ref(label("Some properties about root space decomposition"), supplement: none)\(b), roots always appears in pairs, if let $Delta^(+) subset.eq Delta$ consists of exactly one from each pair, then $sum_(alpha in Delta) alpha\(h\)alpha\(k\)= 2 sum_(alpha in Delta^(+)) alpha\(h\)alpha\(k\)$ #emph[\(3)] By (2), for any $lambda\,mu in frak(h)^(*)$#display-equation($\(lambda\,mu\) & = K\(t_lambda\,t_mu\)\
 & = sum_(alpha in Delta) alpha\(t_lambda\)alpha\(t_beta\)\
 & = sum_(alpha in Delta) K\(t_alpha\,t_lambda\)K\(t_alpha\,t_mu\)\
 & = sum_(alpha in Delta)\(alpha\,lambda\)\(alpha\,mu\)\
 & = 2 sum_(alpha in Delta^(+))\(alpha\,lambda\)\(alpha\,mu\)$)In particular, for any $beta in Delta$, $\(beta\,beta\)= 2 sum_(alpha in Delta^(+))\(alpha\,beta\)^2 arrow.r.double frac(2, \(beta\,beta\)) = sum_(alpha in Delta^(+)) (frac(2\(alpha\,beta\), \(beta\,beta\)))^2$, where $frac(2\(alpha\,beta\), \(beta\,beta\)) in bb(Z)$, thus $0 lt.eq\(beta\,beta\)in bb(Q)$ and equals $0$ iff $\(alpha\,beta\)= 0\,forall alpha in Delta arrow.r.double beta = 0$ by the nondegeneracy of $\(\,\)$, thus $\(\,\)\|_V$ is rational and positive definite on a basis, which implies it is rational and positive definite

]
