== Root system
<root-system>
#statement("Lemma")[
$V$ is a finite dimensional vector space over a field of characteristic $0$, $Delta subset.eq V$ is a finite set such that $V = "Span" Delta$, then for any $alpha in Delta$, there is at most one linear map $s : V arrow.r V$ such that $s^2 = 1$, $s alpha = - alpha\,s\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$

]#label("Delta is finite spaning set of V, then there is at most one s:V->V, maps Delta to Delta, s^2=1 and alpha to -alpha")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Suppose $s\,t$ both satisfy the condition, $s v = v +\(s - 1\)v$, $s\(s - 1\)v = s^2 v - s v = v - s v = -\(s - 1\)v$, thus $\(s - 1\)v in chevron.l alpha chevron.r arrow.r.double s v = v + f\(v\)alpha$, where $f in V^(*)$, similarly, $t v = v + g\(v\)alpha$, where $g in V^(*)$, thus $s t v = s\(v + g\(v\)alpha\)= v + g\(v\)alpha + f\(v + g\(v\)alpha\)alpha = v + g\(v\)alpha + f\(v\)alpha + f\(v\)g\(v\)alpha$, and since $s alpha = alpha + f\(alpha\)alpha = - alpha arrow.r.double f\(alpha\)= - 2$, thus check $\(s t\)^n v = v + n\(f\(v\)- g\(v\)\)alpha$, but $\(s t\)^n = 1$ for some $n$ because $s t$ is just a permutation of $Delta$, thus $f = g arrow.r.double s = t$

]
#aside("Remark")[
$s^2 = 1$ and $s\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$ implies that $s$ is a permutation of $Delta$, note that this definition doesn't involve inner product on $V$, you could see this as a more abstract definition of a reflection

]
#statement("Definition")[
#index-entry("Root system") $V = bb(R)^n$ is the standard Euclidean space with the standard inner product $\(\,\)$, a #strong[root system]#index-entry("Root system") $Delta$ is a finite subset of $V$ satisfying

+ $V = chevron.l Delta chevron.r$

+ If $alpha in Delta$, then the only multiples of $alpha$ are $plus.minus alpha$

+ $s_alpha\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$, where #display-equation($s_alpha\(beta\)= beta - chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r alpha = beta - frac(2\(beta\,alpha\), \(alpha\,alpha\)) alpha$) is the reflection across the hyperplane $H_alpha = { beta in V\|\(beta\,alpha\)= 0 }$

+ $chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = frac(2\(beta\,alpha\), \(alpha\,alpha\)) in bb(Z)$ are called the #strong[Cartan integers]#index-entry("Cartan integers")

]#label("Definition of root system")
#aside("Remark")[
$chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r in bb(Z)$ is the #strong[integrality] condition, and such roots system is called #strong[crystallographic]#index-entry("Crystallographic root system")

$chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = 4 frac(\(alpha\,beta\)^2, \(alpha\,alpha\)\(beta\,beta\)) = 4 cos^2 theta in bb(Z)$ can only be $0\,1\,2\,3\,4$, where $theta$ is the angle between $alpha$ and $beta$, and it is $4$ iff $alpha = plus.minus beta$

#align(center)[
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 6,
    align: (center,center,center,center,center,center,),
    table.header([$chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r$], [0], [1], [2], [3], [4],),
    table.hline(),
    [$theta$], [$plus.minus pi / 2$], [$plus.minus pi / 3$], [$plus.minus pi / 4$], [$plus.minus pi / 6$], [$0$],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

]
$a_(j i) = frac(2\(alpha_i\,alpha_j\), \(alpha_j\,alpha_j\))$ gives a Cartan matrix $A$ with $D_(i j) = frac(delta_(i j), \(alpha_i\,alpha_i\))$, $S_(i j) = 2\(alpha_i\,alpha_j\)$

Conversely, given a generalized Cartan matrix, we can find the corresponding Lie algebra, see Kac-Moody algebra

]
#aside("Remark")[
Thanks to Lemma #ref(label("Delta is finite spaning set of V, then there is at most one s:V->V, maps Delta to Delta, s^2=1 and alpha to -alpha"), supplement: none), $s_alpha$ is the unique linear map $V arrow.r V$ such that $s_alpha\(alpha\)= - alpha\,s_alpha\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$

]
#statement("Definition")[
Let $\(V\,Delta\)$ be a root system, define the coroot#index-entry("coroot") of $alpha in Delta$ to be $alpha^or = frac(2, \(alpha\,alpha\)) alpha$, and let $Delta^or = {alpha^or mid(bar.v) alpha in Delta}$, then $\(V\,Delta^or\)$ is also a root system

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$alpha eq.not plus.minus beta in Delta$. If $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r > 0$, then $alpha - beta in Delta$, if $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r < 0$, then $alpha + beta in Delta$

]#label("alpha not equal to pm beta are roots, (alpha,beta)>0 => alpha-beta is a root")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Note that $s_alpha\(beta\)= beta - chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r alpha in Delta$, $s_beta\(alpha\)= alpha - chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r beta in Delta$, and $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = 4 frac(\(alpha\,beta\)^2, \(alpha\,alpha\)\(beta\,beta\)) = 4 cos^2 theta in bb(Z)$ can only be $0\,1\,2\,3$, where $theta$ is the angle between $alpha$ and $beta$, if $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r > 0 arrow.l.r.double\(alpha\,beta\)> 0$, then $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r = 1\,chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = 1\,2\,3$ or $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r = 2\,3\,chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = 1$, hence either $alpha - beta$ or $beta - alpha$ will be in $Delta$, but then the other will also be in $Delta$. Similarly, if $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r < 0 arrow.l.r.double\(alpha\,beta\)< 0$, then $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r = - 1\,chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = - 1\,- 2\,- 3$ or $chevron.l alpha\,beta chevron.r = - 2\,- 3\,chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = - 1$, hence $alpha + beta in Delta$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$Delta^(+) subset Delta$ is a set of #strong[positive roots] if $alpha in Delta arrow.r.double$ precisely one of $alpha\,- alpha$ is in $Delta^(+)$, and $alpha\,beta in Delta^(+) arrow.r.double alpha + beta in Delta^(+)$, thus we can correspondingly define negative roots $Delta^(-) = Delta\\Delta^(+) = - Delta^(+)$. Any hyperplane $H$ that doesn't intersect $Delta$ separates $Delta$ into $Delta^(+)$ and $Delta^(-)$. $gamma in V$ is called #strong[regular] if $\(gamma\,alpha\)eq.not 0\,forall alpha in Delta$, then $H_gamma = {v in V mid(bar.v) \( gamma \, v \) = 0}$ separates $Delta$ into $Delta^(+)\(gamma\)= {alpha in Delta mid(bar.v) \( gamma \, alpha \) > 0}$ and $Delta^(-)\(gamma\)= {alpha in Delta mid(bar.v) \( gamma \, alpha \) < 0}$. Conversely, given a hyperplane $H$ that doesn't intersect $Delta$ and separates $Delta$ into $Delta^(+)$ and $Delta^(-)$, we can find $gamma in V$ such that $H = H_gamma$, $Delta^(+) = Delta^(+)\(gamma\)$, $Delta^(-) = Delta^(-)\(gamma\)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$gamma in V$ is #strong[dominant] if $\(gamma\,alpha\)gt.eq 0\,forall alpha in Delta^(+)$ and #strong[strictly dominant] if $\(gamma\,alpha\)> 0\,forall alpha in Delta^(+)$

]
#statement("Definition")[
$alpha in Delta^(+)$ is #strong[decomposable] if $alpha = alpha_1 + alpha_2$ for some $alpha_1\,alpha_2 in Delta^(+)$, $alpha$ is a #strong[simple root] of $Delta^(+)$ if it is not decomposable. $S = { alpha_1\,dots.h.c\,alpha_m }$ is a #strong[base] for $Delta^(+)$ if for any $alpha in Delta^(+)$, there are $c_i in bb(Z)_(gt.eq 0)$ such that $alpha = sum_i c_i alpha_i$, which also implies that for any $alpha in Delta^(-) = - Delta^(+)$, there are $c_i in bb(Z)_(lt.eq 0)$ such that $alpha = sum_i c_i alpha_i$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$S = { v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_m }$ are on one side of a hyperplane $H$, and $\(v_i\,v_j\)< 0\,forall i eq.not j$, then $S$ is linearly independent

]#label("v_1,...,v_m on one side of a hyperplane, (v_i,v_j)<0 => v_i linearly independent")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Suppose $sum_(i = 1)^m a_i v_i = 0$ and not all $a_i$'s are zero, then we can rewrite as $sum_(k in K) a_k v_k = sum_(l in L) - a_l v_l$, where $a_k > 0\,forall k in K\,a_l < 0\,forall l in L$, then we have $0 lt.eq (sum_(k in K) a_k v_k \, sum_(l in L) - a_l v_l) = sum_(k in K\,l in L) - a_k a_l\(v_k\,v_l\)< 0$ which is a contradiction

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$S$ is the set of simple roots of $Delta^(+)$, then $S$ is a base for $Delta^(+)$, and $S$ is linearly independent

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
It is obvious that $S$ is a base of $Delta^(+)$ by definition. Suppose $alpha eq.not beta in S$, $alpha eq.not - beta$ is obvious, hence $\(alpha\,beta\)eq.not 0$. If $\(alpha\,beta\)> 0$, then by Lemma #ref(label("alpha not equal to pm beta are roots, (alpha,beta)>0 => alpha-beta is a root"), supplement: none), we have $alpha - beta in Delta$, if $alpha - beta in Delta^(+)$, then $alpha = beta +\(alpha - beta\)$ gives a contradiction, if $alpha - beta in Delta^(-)$, then $beta - alpha in Delta^(+)$ and $beta = alpha +\(beta - alpha\)$ gives a contradiction, therefore $\(alpha\,beta\)< 0$. By lemma #ref(label("v_1,...,v_m on one side of a hyperplane, (v_i,v_j)<0 => v_i linearly independent"), supplement: none), we know $S$ is linearly independent

]
#aside("Remark")[
Given a set of positive roots, there is precisely one base which is the set of simple roots

]
#statement("Lemma")[
$sigma in "GL"\(V\)$, $sigma\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$, then for any $alpha in Delta$, $sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1) = s_(sigma alpha)$, moreover, $chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = chevron.l sigma beta\,sigma alpha chevron.r$

]#label("Conjugacy of roots and Weyl group")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
$sigma^m = 1$ for some $m$ since there are only finitely many choices of maps $Delta arrow.r Delta$, thus $sigma$ is a permutation on $Delta$, hence $sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1)\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$, $(sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1))^2 = 1$ and $sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1) sigma alpha = - sigma alpha$ implies $sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1) = s_(sigma alpha)$ by Lemma #ref(label("Delta is finite spaning set of V, then there is at most one s:V->V, maps Delta to Delta, s^2=1 and alpha to -alpha"), supplement: none). Compare $s_(sigma alpha)\(sigma beta\)= sigma beta - chevron.l sigma beta\,sigma alpha chevron.r sigma alpha$, and $sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1)\(sigma beta\)= sigma s_alpha beta = sigma (beta - chevron.l beta \, alpha chevron.r alpha) = sigma beta - chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r sigma alpha$, we get $chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r = chevron.l sigma beta\,sigma alpha chevron.r$

]
#statement("Definition")[
The #strong[Weyl group]#index-entry("Weyl group") $W$ of $Delta$ is $chevron.l s_alpha\|alpha in Delta chevron.r$. $\|W\|< oo$ since $s_alpha\(Delta\)subset.eq Delta$. $W$ is a subgroup of $O\(V\)$

]
#aside("Remark")[
$W$ is a finite Coxeter group

]
#statement("Definition")[
#strong[Weyl chambers]#index-entry("Weyl chamber") are connected components of $V\\union.big_(alpha in Delta) H_alpha$, as the intersection of open half spaces, Weyl chambers are open convex, each regular $gamma in V$ by definition belongs to precisely one Weyl chamber denote as $C\(gamma\)$, $C\(gamma\)= C\(gamma'\)$ iff $gamma\,gamma'$ are on the side for every hyperplane $H_alpha$ iff $Delta^(+)\(gamma\)= Delta^(+)\(gamma'\)$, thus Weyl chambers are in one to one correspondence with the sets of positive roots, the fundamental Weyl chamber associated to $Delta^(+)$, or rather, associated to $S$ is ${ gamma in V\|\(gamma\,alpha\)> 0\,forall alpha in S }$. For any $sigma in W$, since $\(sigma gamma\,sigma alpha\)=\(gamma\,alpha\)$, $sigma\(Delta^(+)\(gamma\)\)= Delta^(+)\(sigma gamma\)$, $sigma\(S\(gamma\)\)= S\(sigma gamma\)$, $sigma\(C\(gamma\)\)= C\(sigma gamma\)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
If $alpha in Delta^(+)\\S$,then there exists $beta in S$ such that $alpha - beta in Delta^(+)$

]#label("For any positive nonsimple root alpha, there exists simple root beta such that alpha-beta is a positive root")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
It is obvious that $\(alpha\,beta\)eq.not 0\,forall beta in Delta^(+)$, suppose $\(alpha\,beta\)< 0\,forall beta in S$, then by Lemma #ref(label("v_1,...,v_m on one side of a hyperplane, (v_i,v_j)<0 => v_i linearly independent"), supplement: none), $S union { alpha }$ is linearly independent which is impossible, thus $\(alpha\,beta\)> 0$ for some $beta in Delta^(+)$, by Lemma #ref(label("alpha not equal to pm beta are roots, (alpha,beta)>0 => alpha-beta is a root"), supplement: none), $alpha - beta in Delta$, but since $alpha = sum_(alpha_i in S) c_i alpha_i$, and $beta = alpha_j$ for some $j$, since some $c_i > 0$, it necessarily has to be that $alpha - beta =\(c_j - 1\)beta + sum_(i eq.not j) c_i alpha_i in Delta^(+)$

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Each $alpha in Delta^(+)$ can be written as $alpha_1 + dots.h.c + alpha_k$, $alpha_i in S$, here $alpha_i$ may repeat, such that partial sums are all positive roots, i.e. $alpha_i + dots.h.c + alpha_i in Delta^(+)$

]
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Each $alpha in Delta^(+)$ can be written uniquely as a sum of simple roots, by induction on the number of summands and Lemma #ref(label("For any positive nonsimple root alpha, there exists simple root beta such that alpha-beta is a positive root"), supplement: none)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
If $alpha in S$, then $s_alpha$ permutes $Delta^(+)\\{ alpha }$

]#label("If alpha is a simple root, then s_alpha permutes postive roots except alpha")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
For any $beta in Delta^(+)\\{ alpha }$, $beta = sum_(alpha_i in S) c_i alpha_i$, $c_j > 0$ for some $alpha_j eq.not alpha$, then $s_alpha\(beta\)= beta - chevron.l beta\,alpha chevron.r alpha$ still has some positive coefficient, thus it has to be positive, and $s_alpha\(beta\)eq.not alpha = s_alpha\(- alpha\)$

]
#statement("Corollary")[
Let $delta = 1 / 2 sum_(beta in Delta^(+)) beta$, then $s_alpha\(delta\)= delta - alpha\,forall alpha in S$

]#label("delta=half of sum of positive roots, then for any simple root alpha, s_alpha(delta)=delta-alpha")
#statement("Lemma")[
Use $alpha prec 0$ and $alpha succ 0$ to mean positive and negative roots, suppose $alpha_i in S\,i = 1\,dots.h.c\,q$, and denote $s_i := s_(alpha_i)$, if $s_1 dots.h.c s_(q - 1)\(alpha_q\)prec 0$, then $s_1 dots.h.c s_q = s_1 dots.h.c s_(p - 1) s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_(q - 1)$. In particular, suppose $s = s_1 dots.h.c s_q$ is of the smallest length, then $0 prec s_1 dots.h.c s_(q - 1) alpha_q = - s_1 dots.h.c s_q alpha_q arrow.r.double s_1 dots.h.c s_q alpha_q prec 0$

]#label("s=s1...sq is of minimal length => s1...sq(alpha_q) is a negative root")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Write $beta_i = s_i dots.h.c s_(q - 1) alpha_q$, then $beta_q = alpha_q succ 0$, $beta_1 prec 0$, thus there must be some $1 lt.eq p < q$ such that $beta_(p + 1) succ 0$ but $beta_p = s_p beta_(p + 1) prec 0$, by lemma #ref(label("If alpha is a simple root, then s_alpha permutes postive roots except alpha"), supplement: none), thus $beta_(p + 1)$ can only be $alpha_p$, hence by Lemma #ref(label("Conjugacy of roots and Weyl group"), supplement: none), we have#display-equation($ & s_p = s_(alpha_p) = s_(beta_(p + 1)) = s_(s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_(q - 1) alpha_q) =\(s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_(q - 1)\)s_q\(s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_(q - 1)\)^(- 1)\
 & arrow.r.double s_p dots.h.c s_(q - 1) = s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_q\
 & arrow.r.double s_1 dots.h.c s_q = s_1 dots.h.c s_p s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_q = s_(p 1) dots.h.c s_p s_p dots.h.c s_(q - 1) = s_1 dots.h.c s_(p - 1) s_(p + 1) dots.h.c s_(q - 1)$)

]
#statement("Lemma")[
Denote $n\(sigma\)$ the number of positive roots that $sigma$ send to negative. $l\(sigma\)= n\(sigma\)$. Hence there is a unique element $w_compose$ such that $w_compose\(S\)= - S$ of maximal length, and $w_compose^2 = 1$

]
#statement("Theorem")[
+ Let $gamma in V$ be regular, then there exists some $sigma in W$ such that $Delta^(+)\(sigma gamma\)= Delta^(+)$, namely, $W$ acts trasitively on Weyl chambers

+ If $S'$ is a another base, then there exists some $sigma in W$ such that $sigma\(S'\)= S$, namely, $W$ acts trasitively on bases

+ If $alpha$ is any root, then there exists some $sigma in W$ such that $sigma\(alpha\)in S$

+ $W$ is generated by $s_alpha$'s for $alpha in S$

+ If $sigma in W$, then $sigma\(S\)subset.eq S arrow.r.double sigma = 1$, namely, $W$ acts freely and transitively(regularly) on bases(and Weyl chambers)

]#label("Some properties about Weyl group and Weyl chambers")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Let $W' lt.eq W$ be the subgroup of $O\(n\)$ generated by $s_alpha$'s for $alpha in S$

+ Let $delta = 1 / 2 sum_(beta in Delta^(+)) beta$, choose $sigma in W'$ such that $\(sigma gamma\,delta\)$ is the biggest possible, then for any $s_alpha in W'$, due to Corollary #ref(label("delta=half of sum of positive roots, then for any simple root alpha, s_alpha(delta)=delta-alpha"), supplement: none), we have $\(sigma gamma\,delta\)gt.eq\(s_alpha sigma gamma\,delta\)=\(sigma gamma\,s_alpha delta\)=\(sigma gamma\,delta - alpha\)=\(sigma gamma\,delta\)-\(sigma gamma\,alpha\)arrow.r.double\(sigma gamma\,alpha\)gt.eq 0$, since $gamma$ is regular, so is $sigma gamma$, thus $\(sigma gamma\,alpha\)> 0\,forall alpha in S$, $sigma\(C\(gamma\)\)= C\(S\)$, i.e. $W$ acts transitively on Weyl chambers

+ Directly from (a)

+ Thanks to (b), it suffices to prove that each root lies in some base, there exists $gamma in H_alpha\\union.big_(beta in Delta\\{ plus.minus alpha }) H_beta$, and the perturb $gamma$ slightly so that $0 <\(gamma\,alpha\)< lr(|\( gamma \, beta \)|)\,beta in Delta\\{ plus.minus alpha }$, then $alpha in S\(gamma\)$

+ By (c), if $alpha in Delta$, $beta in S$, then there exists $sigma in W'$ such that $sigma alpha = beta$, then $s_beta = s_(sigma alpha) = sigma s_alpha sigma^(- 1)$, thus $s_alpha = sigma^(- 1) s_beta sigma in W'$

+ By (d), $sigma in W$ can be written as $s = s_(alpha_1) dots.h.c s_(alpha_q)\,alpha_i in med S$ and suppose it is of minimal length, by Lemma #ref(label("s=s1...sq is of minimal length => s1...sq(alpha_q) is a negative root"), supplement: none), $sigma\(alpha_q\)prec 0$ contradicting $sigma\(S\)subset.eq S$

]
