#statement("Theorem", title: [Cartan-Killing classification])[
The above Dynkin diagrams classifies simple Lie algebras

]#label("Cartan-Killing classification")
#proof(title: "Proof")[
Consider the #strong[admissible sets] of Euclidean space $V$, $A = { v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_n }$ of linearly independent unit vectors with $\(v_i\,v_j\)lt.eq 0$ and $4\(v_i\,v_j\)^2 in { 0\,1\,2\,3 }$ if $i eq.not j$. Define Coxeter diagram $Gamma_A$ for $A$ to have vertices $v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_n$, and $d_(i j) = 4\(v_i\,v_j\)^2$ edges between $v_i$ and $v_j$ if $i eq.not j$. Assume that $Gamma_A$ is connected

+ The number of vertices in $Gamma_A$ joined by at least one edge is at most $\|A\|- 1$

  $v = v_1 + dots.h.c + v_n eq.not 0$ satisfies $\(v\,v\)= n + 2 sum_(i < j)\(v_i\,v_j\)> 0$, thus $n > - sum_(i < j) 2\(v_i\,v_j\)= sum_(i < j) sqrt(d_(i j)) gt.eq N$, where $N$ is the number of pairs $v_i\,v_j$ such that $d_(i j) gt.eq 1$

+ The graph $Gamma_A$ contains no cycles

  The vectors in a cycle of $Gamma_A$ form an admissible set which a contradicts a)

+ No vertex in $Gamma_A$ has more than $3$ edges

  Let $w$ be a vertex of $Gamma_A$ with adjacent vertices $w_1\,dots.h.c\,w_k$, then $\(w_i\,w_j\)= 0$ for $i eq.not j$. Let $U = "Span"_(bb(R))\(w_1\,dots.h.c\,w_k\,w\)$, and extend ${ w_1\,dots.h.c\,w_k }$ to an orthonormal basis of $U$, say by adjoining $w_0$. Clearly $\(w\,w_0\)eq.not 0$ and $w = sum_(i = 0)^k\(w\,w_i\)w_i$. Hence $1 =\(w\,w\)= sum_(i = 0)^k\(w\,w_i\)^2$, $sum_(i = 1)^k\(w\,w_i\)^2 < 1$, $w$ has no more than $3$ edges

+ If $Gamma_A$ has triple edge, then by c), the $Gamma_A$ can only be $G_2$

+ Assume $Gamma_A$ has a subgraph which is a line along $w_1\,dots.h.c\,w_n$, if we replace this subgraph with $w = w_1 + dots.h.c + w_n$, then it is still an admissible set

  $\(w\,w\)= n + 2 sum_(i = 1)^(n - 1)\(w_i\,w_(i + 1)\)= n -\(n - 1\)= 1$, by d) any vertex $v$ has at most edges linked with one such $w_i$, hence $\(v\,w\)=\(v\,w_i\)$, this gives an admissible set

+ A branch point is a vertex having more than $2$ adjacent vertices, in this case, exactly $3$. $Gamma_A$ has only one double edge, or only one branch point, or neither, but not both. Note that if $Gamma_A$ has no brach points and double edges corresponds to $A_n$

  If $Gamma_A$ has two double edges between $w_1\,w_2$ and $v_1\,v_2$, then they can be linked through a line, by e), we can collapse it into a single vertex, but this will contradict c)

+ If $Gamma_A$ has a subgraph which is a line through $w_1\,dots.h.c\,w_n$, let $w = sum i w_i$, then $\(w\,w\)= frac(n\(n + 1\), 2)$

+ If $Gamma_A$ has a double edge, then $Gamma_A$ is $F_4$ or $B_n$

  By f) we know $Gamma_A$ is a line through $v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_p\,w_q\,dots.h.c\,w_1$, $q gt.eq p gt.eq 1$ with single edges except $v_p\,w_q$, let $v = sum i v_i$, $w = sum i w_i$, then #display-equation($\(v\,w\)^2 =\(p v_p\,q w_q\)^2 = frac(p^2 q^2, 2)$) Since $v\,w$ are linearly independent, by Cauchy Schwarz inequality, we have #display-equation($frac(p^2 q^2, 2) =\(v\,w\)^2 <\(v\,v\)\(w\,w\)= frac(p\(p + 1\)q\(q + 1\), 4)$) Which implies $\(p - 1\)\(q - 1\)< 2$, thus if $p = 1$, then $q$ can be any positive interger, giving $B_n$, if $p = 2$, then $q = 2$, giving $F_4$

+ If $Gamma_A$ has a branch point, then $Gamma_A$ is $D_n$ or $E_6\,E_7\,E_8$

  $Gamma_A$ is has three branch lines $v_1\,dots.h.c\,v_p\,x$ and $w_1\,dots.h.c\,w_q\,x$ together with $z_1\,dots.h.c\,z_r\,x$, $p gt.eq q gt.eq r$, let $v = sum i v_i$, $w = sum i w_i$, $z = sum i z_i$ which are pairwise orthogonal, $hat(v)\,hat(w)\,hat(z)$ be normalized vectors of $v\,w\,z$, and consider $U = "Span"_(bb(R))\(v\,w\,z\,x\)= "Span"_(bb(R))\(hat(v)\,hat(w)\,hat(z)\,x_0\)$, where $x_0$ is a unit vector orthogonal to $v\,w\,z$, then $\(x\,x_0\)eq.not 0$ #display-equation($1 =\(x\,x\)=\(x\,hat(v)\)^2 +\(x\,hat(w)\)^2 +\(x\,hat(z)\)^2 +\(x\,x_0\)^2$) Thus by g) #display-equation($frac(2 p^2, 4 p\(p + 1\)) + frac(2 q^2, 4 q\(q + 1\)) + frac(2 r^2, 4 r\(r + 1\)) < 1$) Hence #display-equation($frac(1, 1 + p) + frac(1, 1 + q) + frac(1, 1 + r) > 1$) and we know that #display-equation($frac(1, 1 + p) lt.eq frac(1, 1 + q) lt.eq frac(1, 1 + r) lt.eq 1 / 2$) Hence $r = 1$ #display-equation($frac(1, 1 + p) + frac(1, 1 + q) > 1 / 2$) If $q = 1$, then $p$ can be any positive integer, giving $D_n$, if $q = 2$, then $p$ can only be $2\,3$ or $4$, giving $E_6\,E_7\,E_8$

]
